var e={"tech:gamesim":{intro:`Game-playing programs teach an electronic computer the rules of a simple game such as Nim, noughts and crosses or draughts, and let a visitor play against it. The games are easy, but they show something startling: a machine that seems to weigh up its moves and answer a human straight away.`,how:`The program stores the board as numbers in memory, tries out possible moves, scores each resulting position by a fixed rule and picks the best. Players enter moves with switches, push-buttons or a telephone dial, and the machine shows its reply on rows of lamps or a small round screen. Looking ahead every possible move would need far more memory than these machines have, so programmers prune the search with clever shortcuts.`,history:`In 1950 Josef Kates showed Bertie the Brain, a noughts-and-crosses machine, at the Canadian National Exhibition in Toronto. In 1951 Ferranti's Nimrod played Nim at the Festival of Britain in London and then at a trade fair in Berlin, where crowds queued to lose to it. By the summer of 1952 Christopher Strachey's draughts program could play a complete game on the Ferranti Mark 1 in Manchester. Also in 1952, Sandy Douglas wrote OXO on the EDSAC at Cambridge, drawing noughts and crosses on a tiny screen for his doctoral thesis on how people and computers interact. In 1952 Arthur Samuel began a draughts program at IBM that improved by playing against copies of itself, and in 1956 it was shown on American television.`,facts:[`OXO's screen was a grid of just 35 by 16 dots (1952).`,`OXO's player chose a square by turning a telephone dial (1952).`,`In 1962 Samuel's draughts program beat Robert Nealey, a strong human player.`]},"tech:scopegame":{intro:`A game on a screen turns a laboratory instrument into a toy. An analog computer works out the path of a bouncing ball, an oscilloscope traces it as a glowing dot, and two players with knobs and buttons hit it back and forth over a net. It is a moving picture that answers the people holding the controls.`,how:`The analog computer does not count: its amplifiers and capacitors behave like the equations of a falling ball, with gravity, air drag and the bounce built in. Switching circuits flick between drawing the ball, the court and the net so fast that the eye sees all three at once. Each player turns a knob to choose the angle of a shot and presses a button to hit, and the ball arcs like a real one.`,history:`In 1947 Thomas Goldsmith and Estle Ray Mann filed a patent for a 'cathode-ray tube amusement device' in which a player aimed at targets on a screen overlay; it was granted in 1948 but never sold. On 18 October 1958 physicist William Higinbotham opened Tennis for Two at Brookhaven National Laboratory's visitors' day in New York state, built with technician Robert Dvorak. In 1958 hundreds of visitors queued to play it on its five-inch screen, many of them school pupils. In 1959 it returned with a bigger screen and settings for the weaker gravity of the Moon and the stronger pull of Jupiter. After the 1959 show it was taken apart and its parts were reused in other equipment.`,facts:[`Tennis for Two was drawn on an oscilloscope screen just five inches across (1958).`,`Its analog computer was a Donner Model 30, a standard laboratory machine (1958).`,`Granted in 1948, the Goldsmith and Mann patent is the earliest known patent for an electronic screen game.`]},"tech:spacewar":{intro:`Real-time interactive graphics let a person steer a picture on a computer screen and see it respond at once, many times a second. Two spaceships duel around a star whose gravity drags them in, firing torpedoes and escaping into hyperspace. It needs a fast computer, a screen it can draw on point by point, and programmers willing to play.`,how:`The program runs in an endless loop: read the players' controls, update each ship's position and speed with simple physics, test whether a torpedo has hit, then plot every object as dots on a round display. Each pass must finish quickly or the motion stutters, so the code is tuned instruction by instruction. Players use homemade control boxes for turning, thrust, firing and hyperspace.`,history:`In 1961 Steve Russell, Martin Graetz and Wayne Wiitanen dreamed up a space duel for the new DEC PDP-1 computer at MIT. By February 1962 Russell had Spacewar! working, and in 1962 Dan Edwards added the gravity of the central star while Peter Samson added an accurate map of the night sky as a backdrop. In 1962 Alan Kotok and Bob Saunders built control boxes, because leaning on the console switches left players with sore elbows. In May 1962 the game was shown at MIT's open house, and copies spread on paper tape to other PDP-1 owners. DEC used it in 1962 to check that new PDP-1s worked. On 19 October 1972 Stanford hosted the Intergalactic Spacewar Olympics, one of the first video game tournaments.`,facts:[`A PDP-1 cost about $120,000 in 1962, so few people could play at home.`,`The prize at the 1972 Spacewar Olympics was a year's subscription to Rolling Stone.`]},"tech:texmap":{intro:`Texture mapping wraps a flat picture around a 3D shape, like wallpaper or a printed label, so a few simple polygons can look like brick, wood or skin. The depth buffer, or z-buffer, records how far away the surface at each pixel is, so nearer objects correctly hide farther ones in whatever order they are drawn. Together they make computer pictures look solid.`,how:`Each corner of a polygon carries coordinates into the texture picture, and as the polygon is filled every pixel looks up its colour at the matching spot. For hiding, the depth buffer keeps one number per pixel; a new pixel is drawn only if it is nearer than the one already there. The idea is simple but hungry, because it needs a memory as large as the whole picture, and memory is very expensive.`,history:`In 1974 Ed Catmull described both texture mapping and the depth buffer in his PhD thesis at the University of Utah, home of Ivan Sutherland and David Evans's famous graphics group. Wolfgang Straßer in Berlin set out the depth buffer independently in 1974. In 1975 Martin Newell at Utah built a 3D model of his teapot to test such methods. In 1976 Jim Blinn and Newell added reflection mapping, and in 1978 Blinn invented bump mapping, faking shiny and rough surfaces with pictures. In 1983 Lance Williams invented mipmapping, storing smaller copies of a texture so distant patterns stop shimmering. In 1986 Catmull co-founded Pixar, which in 1995 released Toy Story.`,facts:[`Catmull's 1972 short film showed a computer-animated model of his own left hand.`,`A depth buffer for a 512 by 512 picture needs over 262,000 stored distances (1974).`,`In 1995 Toy Story became the first feature film made entirely by computer.`]},"tech:coinop":{intro:`A coin-operated video game puts a game in a sturdy cabinet with a screen, controls and a coin box, and leaves it in a bar, café or arcade beside the pinball tables. Players pay a coin for each game, and the owner shares the takings with the site. These machines have no processor: the whole game is wired from logic chips.`,how:`Instead of a program, the game is a circuit board of dozens of simple TTL chips that count along each line of the television picture and switch the beam on wherever a ball or paddle should be. A cheap black-and-white television serves as the monitor. Cabinets are sold to operators, the same firms that place jukeboxes and pinball machines, and they collect the coins each week.`,history:`In September 1971 two Stanford students, Bill Pitts and Hugh Tuck, installed Galaxy Game, a version of Spacewar! running on a PDP-11 minicomputer, in the student union. In November 1971 Nutting Associates released Nolan Bushnell and Ted Dabney's Computer Space, built from logic chips, but many players found it too hard to learn. In June 1972 Bushnell and Dabney founded Atari. In 1972 Allan Alcorn built Pong as a practice project, and the test machine at Andy Capp's Tavern in Sunnyvale stopped working because its coin box was jammed full. By 1973 rival firms were selling copies of Pong faster than Atari could build its own.`,facts:[`Galaxy Game charged 10 cents a game, or a quarter for three (1971).`,`About 1,500 Computer Space cabinets were made (1971).`,`A Pong cabinet could take $35 to $40 a day in quarters (1972).`]},"tech:tvgame":{intro:`A home video game is a small box that plugs into the aerial socket of the family television and turns it into a playing field. Spots of light become players and a ball, steered with dials on wired controllers. There is no sound, no colour and no score on the screen; plastic sheets that cling to the glass supply the scenery.`,how:`Inside are a few dozen transistors and diodes, not a computer. Simple circuits make the dots and lines in step with the television's own sweep, and a tiny transmitter feeds the picture into an unused channel. Swapping a plug-in circuit card rewires the box for a different game, and the cards, dice and paper money in the box keep score.`,history:`In 1966 Ralph Baer, an engineer at the defence firm Sanders Associates in New Hampshire, wrote a four-page proposal for playing games on an ordinary television. By 1968 his small team had built the 'Brown Box', a working prototype with ping-pong, chase and target-shooting games. In 1971 Magnavox licensed the design, and in 1972 the Magnavox Odyssey went on sale for $99.95. In May 1972 Nolan Bushnell tried the Odyssey's tennis game at a public demonstration in California, shortly before Atari made Pong. By 1975 Magnavox had sold about 350,000 Odysseys, held back partly by adverts that suggested it worked only on Magnavox televisions. In 1976 Atari agreed to pay Magnavox $700,000 for a licence to Baer's patents.`,facts:[`The Odyssey came with six circuit cards that played twelve games (1972).`,`The Odyssey ran on batteries and had no sound at all (1972).`]},"tech:pongchip":{intro:`A game on a chip puts a whole television game, paddles, ball, net and score, onto a single custom integrated circuit. What took a board of dozens of chips now fits on one, so a home console can be cheap enough for a Christmas present. The same chip can be bought by anyone, so copies multiply quickly.`,how:`The designers wire the game's logic, counters and scoring into one piece of silicon, so the console needs little more than the chip, a few knobs, a small speaker and a television transmitter. A chip maker can sell one chip with several ball-and-paddle games built in, and dozens of firms build consoles around it that differ only in their plastic cases. Profits fall fast when everyone sells the same thing.`,history:`In 1974 Atari engineers Harold Lee, Allan Alcorn and Bob Brown began shrinking Pong onto one chip for a home version. In 1975 toy shops turned it down, but the department store Sears ordered 150,000 for Christmas and sold them under its Tele-Games name. In 1976 General Instrument released the AY-3-8500, a cheap chip carrying tennis, squash, football and two shooting games. In 1976 Coleco built its Telstar console around that chip and sold more than a million. By 1977 shops were overflowing with near-identical consoles, prices crashed, and makers such as Coleco were left with huge unsold stocks. The 1977 glut pushed the industry towards programmable consoles that could play new games.`,facts:[`Sears sold Home Pong for $98.95 at Christmas 1975.`,`Coleco's Telstar cost about $50 when it appeared in 1976.`]},"tech:cpuarcade":{intro:`A microprocessor game board replaces a game's hard-wired logic with a small general-purpose processor, a program in read-only memory and a picture held in RAM. The game is no longer a circuit but a piece of software, so changing it means rewriting code rather than redesigning a board. Games can now have rules, waves of enemies and high scores.`,how:`The processor runs the game and writes the picture into a frame buffer, a block of memory with one bit for each dot on the screen, which the video circuit reads out to the monitor line by line. Colour comes from strips of tinted film stuck over parts of the screen. Because the processor does all the drawing, the more objects there are, the slower everything moves.`,history:`In 1975 Midway released Gun Fight in the US, Dave Nutting's remake of Taito's Western Gun, rebuilt around an Intel 8080 as the first arcade game run by a microprocessor. In 1976 Midway's Sea Wolf, played through a periscope, used the same kind of board. In 1978 Taito released Tomohiro Nishikado's Space Invaders, which ran on an 8080 and drew a one-bit picture of 256 by 224 dots. In 1978 Space Invaders became a craze in Japan, and Midway licensed it for America that year. Nishikado noticed in 1978 that the invaders sped up as they were shot because the processor had fewer of them to draw, and he kept it as the game's rising tension.`,facts:[`Space Invaders' whole picture fitted in 7,168 bytes, one bit per dot (1978).`,`The Intel 8080 in Space Invaders ran at 2 MHz (1978).`]},"tech:cartridge":{intro:`A ROM cartridge puts a game's program on a read-only memory chip in a plastic case that plugs into a console. The console becomes a small computer with a processor of its own, and each new cartridge gives it a new game. Buyers pay once for the machine and keep buying games for it.`,how:`The cartridge's edge connector joins its memory chip straight to the console's processor, which runs the game directly from the chip with no loading at all. Factory-made ROMs are cheap in large batches, but every copy still needs its own chips, board and case, so each cartridge costs several dollars to make. Memories of a few kilobytes limit how big a game can be.`,history:`In November 1976 Fairchild Semiconductor launched the Video Entertainment System at $169.95, built around its F8 processor, with games on yellow 'Videocart' cartridges. In 1976 its design team was led by Jerry Lawson, one of very few Black engineers in Silicon Valley. In October 1976 Nolan Bushnell sold Atari to Warner Communications for about $28 million to pay for a cartridge console of its own. In January 1977 RCA released the Studio II, with black-and-white games. In 1977 Fairchild renamed its machine the Channel F after Atari's rival appeared. In September 1977 Atari's Video Computer System went on sale at $199 with the Combat cartridge in the box. In 1979 Fairchild left the market and sold the Channel F to Zircon.`,facts:[`The Channel F was the first console with a pause button, marked 'Hold' (1976).`,`Warner paid about $28 million for Atari in 1976.`,`Atari's console had nine games ready when it launched in 1977.`]},"tech:racebeam":{intro:`Racing the beam is how a console with almost no memory draws a picture. There is no room to store a whole screen, so the program must hand the video chip each line of the picture just before the television's electron beam sweeps across it. Programmers count every processor cycle to stay ahead of the beam.`,how:`An American television draws 262 lines sixty times a second. The video chip holds only one line's worth of graphics: a coarse background, two player sprites, two missiles and a ball. While the beam crosses a line, the processor has 76 cycles to change positions, colours and shapes for the next one, then waits for the next line to start. Game logic must fit into the blank moments at the top and bottom of the screen.`,history:`In 1977 Atari engineers Jay Miner and Joe Decuir designed the TIA video chip, code-named Stella, so the Video Computer System could be cheap. The console went on sale in September 1977 with a MOS 6507 processor at 1.19 MHz and just 128 bytes of RAM. In 1980 Warren Robinett's Adventure hid a secret room with his name in it, because Atari gave its designers no credit. In 1982 David Crane's Pitfall! fitted 255 jungle screens into a 4 KB cartridge by generating them from a number sequence. Also in 1982, Atari's rushed version of Pac-Man flickered badly because the chip could show only two sprites at once. Atari stopped making the console, renamed the 2600 in 1982, in 1992.`,facts:[`The 6507 could reach only 8 KB of memory, so cartridges held 4 KB without tricks (1977).`,`About 30 million Atari 2600 consoles had been sold by 1992.`]},"tech:textadv":{intro:`A text adventure is a game made only of words. The computer describes a place, the player types short commands such as GO NORTH or TAKE LAMP, and the program replies with what happens. Players map caves, solve puzzles and collect treasure with nothing but their imagination, a terminal and a sheet of paper.`,how:`The world is a set of rooms joined by exits, plus objects and rules about them. A parser breaks each command into a verb and a noun and looks them up in its vocabulary. Puzzles are conditions to be met: the snake will not move until something scares it away. On a big timesharing computer the whole game fits in a few dozen kilobytes.`,history:`In 1975 and 1976 Will Crowther, a programmer at BBN and a keen caver, wrote Colossal Cave Adventure on a PDP-10, modelling its passages on Kentucky's Mammoth Cave. In 1977 Don Woods at Stanford found the game, asked Crowther's permission and expanded it with fantasy treasures and puzzles. In 1977 four MIT students, Tim Anderson, Marc Blank, Bruce Daniels and Dave Lebling, wrote Zork, whose parser understood commands such as KILL TROLL WITH SWORD. In 1978 Scott Adams sold Adventureland on cassette for the TRS-80 home computer. In 1979 the Zork team founded Infocom, and in 1984 it published The Hitchhiker's Guide to the Galaxy with Douglas Adams. In 1986 Activision bought Infocom.`,facts:[`Woods's 1977 version of Adventure had a perfect score of 350 points.`,`In 1980 Zork was split into three games to fit home computers' small memories.`]},"tech:mud":{intro:`A multi-user dungeon, or MUD, is a text adventure in which the other characters are real people typing on other terminals at the same moment. Players explore, fight, trade and chat in a shared world of words, and make friends and enemies there. The world lives on one computer, and anyone who can connect to it can join.`,how:`One program runs the world and keeps track of every player's location, belongings and score. Each player's terminal sends commands to it, and the program sends back descriptions, including what anyone else in the same room is doing. Because many players share one machine, the game must be quick and frugal with memory. Players who reach the top rank become 'wizards' with powers over the game itself.`,history:`In 1978 Roy Trubshaw, a student at the University of Essex, wrote the first MUD on the university's DEC PDP-10. Shared games had already run since 1973 on the PLATO teaching system at the University of Illinois, where Empire and, in 1975, dnd drew crowds of students. In 1980 Trubshaw handed MUD to fellow student Richard Bartle, who built most of its world. In 1980 Essex connected its computer to outside networks, and players from other countries began dialling in at night. In 1987 CompuServe offered the game to its subscribers as British Legends. In 1989 TinyMUD made conversation and building, not fighting, the point of the game.`,facts:[`In 1996 Bartle sorted MUD players into four types: achievers, explorers, socialisers and killers.`,`Outsiders could play MUD only at night, when Essex's computer was not needed for work (1980).`]},"tech:sprites":{intro:`Hardware sprites are small moving pictures that the graphics chip lays over the background while the screen is being drawn. The processor only says where each sprite is and which picture it shows, instead of redrawing it in memory every frame. With colour palettes added, games become bright, smooth and full of moving characters.`,how:`The background is built from a grid of tiles, small square pictures reused across the screen. As each line is scanned, the chip checks which sprites overlap it, fetches their pixels and draws them on top, leaving see-through areas clear. A chip can show only a few sprites on one line, so too many side by side start to flicker. Each sprite or tile picks its colours from a small palette.`,history:`In 1979 Atari's 400 and 800 home computers drew moving objects as 'player-missile graphics', overlays much like sprites. In 1979 Namco's Galaxian used sprite hardware to show swooping aliens in many colours. In 1979 Texas Instruments engineers called such objects 'sprites' in their TMS9918 video chip, which could show 32 of them. In 1982 the VIC-II chip in the Commodore 64 offered eight sprites that could be stretched and could detect when they touched. In 1983 the picture chip in Nintendo's Famicom handled 64 sprites, up to eight on one line. In 1985 the Amiga added a 'blitter' that copied large blocks of graphics quickly.`,facts:[`The TMS9918 could show only four sprites on any one line (1979).`,`Galaxian was among the first arcade games with full-colour graphics (1979).`]},"tech:thirdparty":{intro:`Third-party publishing means games for a console made by a company that did not make the console. Independent studios write and sell their own cartridges, competing with the console maker's games on the same shelves. Designers get their names on the box and a share of the money, and buyers get far more games to choose from.`,how:`A third-party studio learns how the console works, often by studying it from the outside, writes its game and pays a factory to make the cartridges. It sells through the same toy and electronics shops as the console maker. With no licence needed, nothing stops poor games from flooding the shelves, and the console maker earns nothing from them.`,history:`In 1979 four of Atari's best programmers, David Crane, Alan Miller, Bob Whitehead and Larry Kaplan, left after asking in vain for credit and royalties. On 1 October 1979 they founded Activision with Jim Levy, a record-industry executive, and in 1980 its first cartridges named their designers on the box. In 1980 Atari sued Activision for using its trade secrets, and in 1982 the case was settled with Activision paying Atari a royalty. In 1981 more former Atari and Mattel staff founded Imagic. In 1982 Activision's Pitfall! became one of the console's biggest hits. By 1983 dozens of firms, among them a breakfast-cereal maker and a pet-food company, were selling Atari games, many of them poor.`,facts:[`Pitfall! sold more than 4 million copies after its release in 1982.`,`Activision's first four games were Dragster, Boxing, Checkers and Fishing Derby (1980).`]},"tech:vector":{intro:`A vector display draws its picture the way a pen draws on paper: the electron beam moves straight from point to point, tracing lines, instead of scanning row after row. The lines are bright and sharp at any angle, with no jagged steps. But the screen cannot fill in solid shapes, so worlds are made of glowing outlines.`,how:`A special circuit, the vector generator, reads a list of line segments from memory and steers the beam with magnetic coils, while the processor updates the list for each frame. The more lines a picture has, the longer it takes to draw, so scenes must stay simple. The monitors run hot and need careful adjustment, and the early ones are black and white.`,history:`In 1977 Cinematronics released Space Wars, Larry Rosenthal's arcade version of Spacewar! and the first vector arcade hit. In August 1979 Atari's Lunar Lander used its new vector system, and in November 1979 Asteroids followed, by Lyle Rains and Ed Logg. In 1980 Asteroids sold so well that Atari stopped making Lunar Lander and fitted Asteroids boards into its leftover cabinets. In 1980 Battlezone used vectors for a 3D tank battle, and in 1981 Tempest brought colour vectors. In 1982 the Vectrex went on sale as a home console with its own built-in vector screen. In 1983 Atari's Star Wars arcade game flew players down the Death Star trench in colour vectors.`,facts:[`Asteroids sold more than 50,000 cabinets and became Atari's biggest arcade hit (1980).`,`The Vectrex cost $199 in 1982, screen included.`]},"tech:gamevm":{intro:`A game virtual machine is an imaginary computer invented just to run games. The game is compiled once into code for this pretend machine, and a small interpreter program is written for each real computer. With dozens of incompatible home computers on sale, one game file can then reach all of them.`,how:`The virtual machine defines its own instructions, memory layout and ways to print text, read the keyboard or show graphics. The interpreter on each real computer reads those instructions one at a time and carries them out with that computer's own hardware. It runs slower than code written for the machine itself, but it is far cheaper than rewriting a game for every computer, and it can load parts of a large game from disk as needed.`,history:`In 1979 Joel Berez and Marc Blank designed Infocom's Z-machine so the huge PDP-10 Zork could run on home computers. In 1980 Zork I shipped for the TRS-80 and Apple II on the Z-machine. By 1985 Infocom's games ran on about two dozen kinds of computer, each needing only its own interpreter. In 1984 Sierra built its AGI engine for King's Quest on the IBM PCjr. In 1987 Ron Gilbert and Aric Wilmunder at Lucasfilm Games created SCUMM for the adventure Maniac Mansion. In 1993 Graham Nelson released Inform, a compiler that let hobbyists write new Z-machine games. In 2001 the ScummVM project began reviving old Lucasfilm adventures on new machines.`,facts:[`SCUMM stands for Script Creation Utility for Maniac Mansion (1987).`,`Sierra's AGI stood for Adventure Game Interpreter (1984).`]},"tech:procgen":{intro:`Procedural generation means storing a recipe for a game world instead of the world itself. A program builds dungeons, landscapes or whole star systems from rules and random numbers, so a tiny memory can hold a huge world. A new random seed gives a new world each time, so no two games need be the same.`,how:`A pseudo-random number generator turns a starting number, the seed, into a long sequence that looks random but repeats exactly whenever the same seed is used. The program uses these numbers to place rooms, corridors, monsters and treasure according to rules that keep the result playable. Saving a whole world then means saving only its seed.`,history:`In 1978 Don Worth's Beneath Apple Manor for the Apple II built a fresh dungeon for every game. In 1980 Michael Toy and Glenn Wichman wrote Rogue at the University of California, Santa Cruz, drawing random dungeon levels out of letters and symbols. In 1982 Carol Shaw's River Raid for the Atari VCS generated an endless river from a number sequence, all in a tiny cartridge. In 1983 Rogue was included with Berkeley's version of Unix and spread to universities everywhere. In 1984 Elite generated eight galaxies of 256 star systems each from a handful of seed numbers. In 1986 The Sentinel offered 10,000 different landscapes on cassette.`,facts:[`Elite's authors could have generated about 282 trillion galaxies but kept just eight (1984).`,`Elite fitted its whole universe into 32 KB of memory (1984).`]},"tech:lcdgame":{intro:`An LCD handheld is a pocket game built from the liquid-crystal display and cheap chip of a calculator. The pictures are fixed shapes printed into the display, and the game switches each one on or off, so a character seems to hop from pose to pose. A battery lasts for months, and the game doubles as a clock and alarm.`,how:`Every possible position of every character is a separate segment of the display, drawn in advance by the designer, like the bars of a calculator's digits. A tiny four-bit chip of the kind used in calculators runs the game and darkens the right segments. Nothing else can be shown, but the parts cost very little and use almost no power.`,history:`In 1976 Mattel's Auto Race used red LED lights as a handheld game. In 1979 Milton Bradley's Microvision put swappable cartridges on a small LCD screen. In 1979, the story goes, Nintendo engineer Gunpei Yokoi watched a bored businessman on a bullet train fiddling with a pocket calculator and thought of a game the same size. In April 1980 Nintendo released the first Game & Watch, Ball, using display parts from Sharp. In 1982 the Donkey Kong Game & Watch opened like a book to show two screens and introduced a cross-shaped direction pad. Nintendo made about 60 Game & Watch titles until 1991.`,facts:[`The Microvision's screen was just 16 by 16 dots (1979).`,`Nintendo sold about 43 million Game & Watch units by 1991.`]},"tech:mascot":{intro:`A game character, or mascot, gives players a face to recognise and care about instead of a spaceship or a paddle. A simple, cute design reads clearly on a blocky screen and works just as well on T-shirts, lunchboxes and cartoons. A character turns a game into a brand that can be sold again and again.`,how:`Designers work with a few dozen pixels and a handful of colours, so characters need bold shapes: a round body, big eyes, a cap instead of hair, a moustache instead of a mouth. Personality comes from animation and behaviour; in a maze game, each enemy can chase in its own way. Licences for toys, records and television shows earn money far beyond the arcade.`,history:`In May 1980 Namco released Toru Iwatani's Puck Man in Japan, a maze game designed to appeal to women as well as men. In October 1980 Midway brought it to America as Pac-Man, and its four ghosts, Blinky, Pinky, Inky and Clyde, each hunted the player in a different way. In 1981 Shigeru Miyamoto's Donkey Kong introduced Jumpman, a carpenter in a cap and dungarees. In 1982 Pac-Man had a hit record, Pac-Man Fever, and a Saturday-morning cartoon in the US. By 1982 Nintendo was calling Jumpman 'Mario', reportedly after the landlord of its American warehouse. In 1991 Sega launched Sonic the Hedgehog to give its console a mascot to rival Mario.`,facts:[`Iwatani said Pac-Man's shape came from a pizza with one slice missing (1980).`,`Pac-Man sold nearly 300,000 arcade cabinets between 1981 and 1987.`,`The song Pac-Man Fever reached number 9 in the US charts (1982).`]},"tech:wireframe":{intro:`Wireframe 3D shows solid objects as outlines of their edges, like models made of wire. The computer stores each object as a list of corner points, turns them in space with trigonometry and projects them onto the flat screen. The result is crude but truly three-dimensional: drive round a tank and you see its other side.`,how:`Each corner has three coordinates. To show it, the program rotates and moves the points relative to the viewer, divides each by its distance so faraway things look smaller, and draws a line between connected corners. Removing edges that a solid face would hide takes extra work. Processors without a multiply instruction lean on tables of sines and other shortcuts.`,history:`In 1963 Ivan Sutherland's Sketchpad at MIT let a user draw line pictures on a screen and turn them. In 1979 subLOGIC's Flight Simulator brought wireframe 3D to the Apple II. In November 1980 Atari's Battlezone put players inside a tank on a vector screen, looking out through a periscope viewer. In 1981 the US Army asked Atari for a training version of Battlezone. In 1982 Microsoft Flight Simulator, built on subLOGIC's work, appeared for the IBM PC. In September 1984 David Braben and Ian Bell's Elite for the BBC Micro drew its spaceships with hidden lines removed, which few home games had managed.`,facts:[`Sketchpad let its user draw straight onto the screen with a light pen (1963).`,`Elite ran on a 2 MHz processor with no multiply instruction (1984).`]},"tech:copyprot":{intro:`Copy protection tries to stop players from copying a game and handing it out. Disks are made deliberately strange so ordinary copying programs fail, and games ask questions that only the owner of the box can answer. Every scheme is soon beaten by crackers, who compete to remove it first and sign their work.`,how:`Disk schemes write sectors in odd sizes, put data between the normal tracks or include deliberate errors that a copy will not reproduce, and the game checks for them as it starts. Other schemes need something printed: a word from a page of the manual, a symbol on a card wheel, or codes on dark paper that photocopiers cannot copy. Honest buyers often suffer more than pirates.`,history:`In 1980 Apple II publishers began using non-standard disk formats to block copying. By 1981 copying programs such as Locksmith and Copy II Plus were sold openly to beat them. In 1981 the British music industry launched its 'Home Taping Is Killing Music' campaign as cassette copying spread to games. By 1985 cracking groups on the Commodore 64 were adding flashy intro screens to the games they freed. In 1985 Elite on the ZX Spectrum used Lenslok, a plastic prism that unscrambled a code on the screen. In 1990 The Secret of Monkey Island came with Dial-a-Pirate, a cardboard code wheel. In 2014 Denuvo appeared, aiming to protect a game only through its first weeks on sale.`,facts:[`Lenslok often failed because the code's size depended on the player's TV (1985).`,`Dial-a-Pirate asked players to line up pirate faces to find the code (1990).`]},"tech:scrolling":{intro:`Scrolling lets the game world slide past the screen as the player moves, so a level can be far larger than one screen. The world is stored as a map of small square tiles, each a little picture reused many times. Only the edge coming into view needs new drawing, so long, detailed levels fit in small memories.`,how:`A tile map records which tile goes in each square of the level. The graphics chip has registers that shift the whole background by a few pixels at a time, and the program writes a fresh column or row of tiles into the hidden edge before it slides into view. Machines without such hardware must redraw the screen every frame, which is too slow for smooth motion.`,history:`In February 1981 Williams released Eugene Jarvis's Defender, a shooter whose world scrolled sideways past the player, with a radar map of the whole planet. In 1981 Konami's Scramble scrolled over changing ground, and in 1982 Namco's Xevious scrolled upwards. In 1982 Irem's Moon Patrol used parallax scrolling, moving distant hills slower than near ones to suggest depth. In September 1985 Nintendo's Super Mario Bros scrolled across 32 levels on the Famicom. In September 1990 John Carmack showed smooth scrolling on ordinary PCs with EGA graphics, and in December 1990 the trick powered Commander Keen.`,facts:[`Defender used a joystick and five buttons, a lot for its time (1981).`,`Super Mario Bros, released in 1985, sold about 40 million copies.`]},"tech:dpad":{intro:`The D-pad, or directional pad, is a flat cross-shaped button pressed with the thumb to move up, down, left or right. It replaces a fragile joystick with something thin, cheap and tough enough to survive years in a pocket or under a child's thumb. It is precise for games built on four directions.`,how:`Under the plus-shaped rocker sit four contacts; tilting the cross presses one or two of them, giving eight directions. A pivot in the centre stops opposite directions being pressed together. The controller stays flat, so the other thumb is free for the action buttons, and there is no stick to snap off.`,history:`In 1979 Mattel's Intellivision used a flat disc that sensed 16 directions under the thumb. In 1982 Gunpei Yokoi put a cross-shaped pad on Nintendo's Donkey Kong Game & Watch, because a joystick would not fit in a folding handheld. In July 1983 Nintendo's Famicom console used D-pads on both of its wired controllers. In 1985 the pad reached America with the Nintendo Entertainment System. In 1989 the Game Boy carried the same cross into pockets everywhere. In 2008 Nintendo received a US Technology and Engineering Emmy award for the D-pad.`,facts:[`The NES pad had just two action buttons, plus Select and Start (1985).`,`The Famicom's second controller had a microphone instead of Select and Start (1983).`]},"tech:chipmusic":{intro:`A sound chip is a tiny programmable synthesiser inside a computer or console. Instead of storing recordings, which would fill the memory in seconds, the game sends the chip notes and settings, and the chip makes the tones itself. Whole soundtracks fit in a few kilobytes, and composers learn to write for three or four voices.`,how:`Each voice makes a simple wave, square, triangle, sawtooth or noise, at a pitch and volume set by the program, often with an envelope that shapes how a note starts and fades. Better chips add filters, or FM synthesis, which bends one wave with another to make bell and brass sounds. A music routine in the game feeds the chip new values many times a second.`,history:`In 1978 General Instrument's AY-3-8910 offered three square-wave voices and noise, and it spread to arcade machines and home computers. In 1982 Bob Yannes designed the SID chip for the Commodore 64, with three voices, several waveforms and an analogue filter. In 1983 Nintendo's Famicom built its sound into the processor: two pulse voices, a triangle, noise and short samples. In 1985 Koji Kondo's music for Super Mario Bros showed how much those few voices could do. In 1988 Sega's Mega Drive used a Yamaha FM synthesis chip. In 1990 a Sony-designed sound chip in the Super Famicom played sampled instruments.`,facts:[`The Commodore 64 cost $595 when it launched in 1982.`,`The SID chip's three voices could each span eight octaves (1982).`]},"tech:polygons":{intro:`Filled polygons build 3D objects from flat faces, usually triangles or four-sided shapes, painted in solid colour instead of shown as wire outlines. Faces turned away from the viewer are dropped, and the rest are drawn so nearer ones cover farther ones. It takes many times the arithmetic of wireframe, so early games keep to a few hundred faces.`,how:`After rotating and projecting each corner, the program fills every visible face line by line between its left and right edges. The simplest way to hide surfaces is the painter's algorithm: sort faces by distance and paint the farthest first. Shading each face by its angle to a light makes shapes look solid. Fast multiplication matters most, and some machines add maths chips just for this.`,history:`In 1983 Atari's I, Robot, designed by Dave Theurer, became the first arcade game drawn with filled 3D polygons, but few were sold. In 1987 Incentive Software's Freescape engine filled whole explorable worlds on home computers in Driller. In 1989 Atari's Hard Drivin' let players race a polygon car round a polygon track. In 1992 Sega's Model 1 arcade board, packed with maths chips, ran Virtua Racing in smooth full 3D. In 1993 Nintendo's Star Fox carried Argonaut's Super FX chip inside the cartridge to draw polygons on the Super Nintendo.`,facts:[`The Super FX chip ran about three times faster than the console's own processor (1993).`,`Hard Drivin' had a steering wheel that pushed back against the player (1989).`]},"tech:lockout":{intro:`A lockout chip is a small security chip inside a console that must trade secret signals with a matching chip in every cartridge before a game will run. Without the handshake, the console keeps resetting itself. It lets the console maker decide who may publish games, how many, and on what terms.`,how:`Both chips run the same tiny program and send each other a stream of values; if the console's chip ever sees a wrong answer, it holds the main processor in reset. Only the console maker supplies the cartridge chips, so publishers must order their cartridges through it, pay a fee for every copy and accept its rules. Approved games carry a seal of quality on the box.`,history:`In 1983 the North American games market collapsed under a flood of poor games, and Atari buried unsold cartridges in a landfill in Alamogordo, New Mexico. In October 1985 Nintendo launched its Entertainment System in New York with the 10NES lockout chip and a toy robot accessory, so that shops would see a toy rather than another games console. From 1985 Nintendo allowed each licensed publisher only five NES games a year, and in 1988 Konami got round the rule by setting up a second label, Ultra Games. In 1988 Atari Games' Tengen division sold unlicensed NES cartridges that defeated the chip. In 1992 a US appeals court ruled against Tengen, partly because it had obtained Nintendo's code from the US Copyright Office by misleading it.`,facts:[`US sales of home video games fell by about 97 per cent between 1983 and 1985.`,`In 2014 diggers in Alamogordo found the buried Atari cartridges, among them copies of E.T.`]},"tech:saves":{intro:`Saved games let a player stop and carry on later without starting again from the beginning. Consoles have no disks, so players copy down long passwords that rebuild their progress, or the cartridge carries a little memory kept alive by a battery. Games can now be far longer than one sitting.`,how:`A password packs the important facts, such as the level, items and score, into a string of letters that the game decodes. Battery-backed memory is a small RAM chip that keeps its contents as long as a coin-sized lithium cell supplies a trickle of power. Computers write saves to floppy disks instead, and some consoles add disk drives or plug-in memory cards.`,history:`In 1980 Infocom's Zork let home computer players save their position to disk. In February 1986 Nintendo's The Legend of Zelda launched on the Famicom Disk System in Japan, saving to rewritable floppy disks. In 1986 Metroid also saved to disk in Japan, but its 1987 American cartridge used long passwords, and players passed round famous ones such as JUSTIN BAILEY. In 1987 the American Zelda came on a gold cartridge with a battery and memory chip inside, so it could save without a disk. In December 1994 Sony's PlayStation stored saves on plug-in memory cards. In 2001 the Xbox became the first major console with a hard disk built in.`,facts:[`A PlayStation memory card held 128 KB, split into 15 save slots (1994).`,`The Xbox's built-in hard disk held 8 GB (2001).`]},"tech:shareware":{intro:`Shareware is software that anyone may copy and pass on freely, trusting that people who like it will pay. For games, the trick is to give away the first episode and sell the rest by post. Copying, the industry's worst enemy, becomes free advertising for small teams with no publisher.`,how:`The free episode spreads on bulletin boards, magazine cover disks and university file servers. At its end a screen tells players how to order the full set, usually by posting a cheque or calling a number, and the remaining episodes arrive on floppy disks. With no shop, box or publisher taking a share, small teams keep most of the money.`,history:`In 1982 Andrew Fluegelman released his PC-Talk program on a pay-if-you-like basis, and in 1983 Bob Wallace called his PC-Write 'shareware'. In 1987 Scott Miller's company Apogee released Kingdom of Kroz with the first episode free and the others for sale. In December 1990 Commander Keen, published by Apogee the same way, earned its young makers enough to quit their jobs, and in February 1991 they founded id Software. In May 1992 Wolfenstein 3D followed as shareware. On 10 December 1993 id put the first episode of Doom on a University of Wisconsin server, and the rush of downloaders overwhelmed it. By 1995, by some estimates, about 10 million people had played Doom, most of them on free copies.`,facts:[`Kingdom of Kroz was Apogee's first game (1987).`,`Doom's free first episode had nine levels (1993).`]},"tech:cdgames":{intro:`A CD-ROM holds about 650 megabytes, hundreds of times more than a game cartridge, and costs very little to press. Games can carry recorded music, spoken dialogue, photographs and filmed video. The catch is speed: the disc reads slowly and finds its place even more slowly, so players wait while each part loads.`,how:`Data is stored as microscopic pits along a spiral track, read by a laser as the disc spins. A single-speed drive reads 150 kilobytes a second, so a game streams what it needs into memory and hides the loading behind menus and short films. Discs are cheap to make in a factory and impossible for buyers to rewrite, so publishers can print large runs at low cost.`,history:`In 1985 Philips and Sony published the Yellow Book standard for putting computer data on compact discs. In December 1988 NEC's PC Engine CD-ROM² became the first CD add-on for a games console. In 1991 Sega's Mega-CD and Philips's CD-i arrived, and in 1992 Night Trap on the Mega-CD was made almost entirely of filmed actors. In 1993 The 7th Guest and Myst persuaded millions of PC owners to buy CD-ROM drives. In December 1994 Sony's PlayStation launched in Japan with all its games on CD. In 1997 Final Fantasy VII filled three discs with its world and films.`,facts:[`Myst was the best-selling PC game until The Sims overtook it in 2002.`,`A single CD held as much as about 450 floppy disks (1988).`]},"tech:raycast":{intro:`Raycasting draws a fast 3D view from a flat map. For each column of the screen the program casts one imaginary ray from the player across a grid of walls and draws a vertical strip of wall where it hits: tall if near, short if far. Ordinary PCs can show textured corridors that move smoothly.`,how:`The map is a grid of squares, each either empty or wall. A ray is traced square by square until it meets a wall; the distance sets the strip's height, and the exact spot where it struck chooses which column of the wall's picture to draw. Floors and ceilings are flat colours, every wall stands upright at right angles, and no room can sit on top of another.`,history:`In April 1991 id Software's Hovertank 3D used raycasting for plain coloured walls, and in November 1991 Catacomb 3-D added textures. In March 1992 Blue Sky Productions' Ultima Underworld went further, with sloping floors and the freedom to look up and down. In May 1992 id released Wolfenstein 3D as shareware through Apogee, drawing textured walls at speed on ordinary 386 PCs. In September 1992 id followed it with Spear of Destiny, sold in shops. In 1994 Wolfenstein 3D reached the Super Nintendo, with its guard dogs turned into giant rats.`,facts:[`Wolfenstein 3D's levels were grids of 64 by 64 squares (1992).`,`Wolfenstein 3D drew its world in 256 colours on VGA graphics (1992).`]},"tech:gfxapi":{intro:`A 3D graphics API is a standard set of drawing commands, such as 'draw these triangles with this texture and this light'. The game speaks the standard, and each graphics card maker supplies a driver that translates it for its own chips. A game no longer has to be rewritten for every card on the market.`,how:`The programmer sends lists of corner points, textures and settings through the API, and the driver turns them into instructions for the card's hardware, or does the work on the main processor if the card cannot. The API hides the differences between makers but adds some overhead, and new features arrive only when the standard's owners agree to add them.`,history:`In 1992 Silicon Graphics turned its own IRIS GL into OpenGL 1.0, an open standard overseen by a board of companies. In 1995 Microsoft bought RenderMorphics, a small London firm, and in June 1996 shipped its engine as Direct3D in DirectX 2.0. In 1996 3dfx launched Glide, a simpler interface for its own Voodoo cards, which many early 3D games used. In December 1996 John Carmack publicly criticised Direct3D and backed OpenGL for his games. In December 2000 NVIDIA bought 3dfx's assets, and Glide faded away. In February 2016 the Khronos Group released Vulkan, which gave programmers much closer control of the hardware.`,facts:[`Microsoft first released DirectX in 1995 to make Windows a games platform.`,`Vulkan grew out of AMD's Mantle interface, announced in 2013.`]},"tech:bsp":{intro:`A binary space partitioning tree cuts a level in two, then cuts each half again, and again, until every piece is simple. From any viewpoint, walking through the tree gives the walls in exact order from nearest to farthest, with no sorting at all. The heavy work is done once, before the game ships.`,how:`Each cut is a plane, or in a flat map a line, taken from one of the walls. To draw a frame, the program starts at the top of the tree and always visits the side of each cut that the player stands on first, so nearer walls come out before farther ones. Because the tree is built in advance, walls cannot move sideways; doors and lifts can only slide up and down.`,history:`In 1969 Robert Schumacker and colleagues at General Electric used a similar idea to sort scenes for flight simulators. In 1980 Henry Fuchs, Zvi Kedem and Bruce Naylor described BSP trees at the SIGGRAPH graphics conference. In 1993 John Carmack read about them while hunting for a way to make Doom fast enough. In December 1993 Doom drew its levels from a BSP tree made by a separate 'node builder' program. In 1996 Quake used fully 3D BSP trees, along with precomputed lists of which areas could see which others. In 1998 Half-Life and Unreal also built their levels around BSP trees.`,facts:[`Doom's levels were really flat maps with a floor and ceiling height for each area (1993).`,`Building Quake's visibility lists for one level could take hours of computing (1996).`]},"tech:lanplay":{intro:`Networked multiplayer links several computers so friends can play in the same game world at once, each at their own screen. To save bandwidth, every machine runs a full copy of the game and sends only what its player did. As long as all the copies calculate exactly alike, they stay in step.`,how:`In lockstep, every machine waits each turn for every other player's inputs, then all advance the game by the same step. It needs very little data, so it works even over slow modems, but one slow connection holds everyone back, and the tiniest difference in arithmetic makes the copies drift apart. Players connect through office networks, serial cables or modems.`,history:`In 1974 Maze War let players on linked Imlac computers at NASA's Ames Research Center hunt each other through a 3D maze. In 1987 MIDI Maze on the Atari ST linked up to 16 machines through their music ports. In December 1993 Doom let four players fight over an office network, and John Romero called the mode 'deathmatch'. In 1994 Doom's network traffic slowed so many office and university networks that some banned the game. In 1997 Ensemble Studios' Age of Empires used lockstep to keep hundreds of units in step over dial-up modems.`,facts:[`In 2001 Age of Empires' engineers wrote up their method as '1500 Archers on a 28.8'.`,`Doom's first network mode allowed four players (1993).`]},"tech:mods":{intro:`Mod tools let players change a game or build new levels for it and share them with others. Shipping an editor, or simply keeping the game's data in files that fans can open, turns players into designers. Free levels keep a game alive for years, and a good modification can grow into a whole new game.`,how:`The game keeps its levels, pictures, sounds and rules in separate data files rather than locked inside the program. Fans write editors, or receive official ones, to change those files and pass on only their changes. Some games add a scripting language, so modders can alter how monsters, weapons and rules behave and not only how a level looks.`,history:`In 1983 Bill Budge's Pinball Construction Set and the level editor in Lode Runner let players design their own boards. Also in 1983, fans turned Castle Wolfenstein into a parody called Castle Smurfenstein. In 1994 Doom players began sharing their own levels as WAD files, built with fan-made editors. In 1996 Quake came with QuakeC, a language for changing the rules, and fans made Team Fortress that year. In 1999 Minh Le and Jess Cliffe released Counter-Strike as a Half-Life mod, and in 2000 Valve hired them and bought it. In 2003 a Warcraft III map called Defense of the Ancients started a new kind of team battle game.`,facts:[`Doom's WAD files were said to stand for 'Where's All the Data?' (1993).`,`Valve sold Counter-Strike in shops as a boxed game in 2000.`]},"tech:ratings":{intro:`Age ratings put a label on a game's box saying who it suits, from young children to adults only, with notes such as 'animated violence'. The games industry sets up its own rating body, hoping to reassure parents and head off laws written by governments. Shops can then refuse to sell adult games to children.`,how:`Publishers send a rating board video of the game and answers to questions about violence, language, sex and gambling. Trained raters agree on a category, and its symbol must appear on the box and in adverts. The system is voluntary in law but hard to avoid, because big shops will not stock unrated games.`,history:`In September 1993 Mortal Kombat came to home consoles with its blood and gory finishing moves, and Night Trap drew complaints over its filmed scenes. On 9 December 1993 Senators Joe Lieberman and Herb Kohl held hearings in Washington on violent games and threatened government action. In 1993 Sega set up its own Videogame Rating Council. In September 1994 the industry founded the Entertainment Software Rating Board, with categories from Early Childhood to Adults Only. In 1994 Germany founded its own rating body, the USK. In 2003 most of Europe adopted the PEGI system. In 2011 the US Supreme Court struck down a California law banning sales of violent games to minors.`,facts:[`In 1998 the ESRB renamed its 'Kids to Adults' rating 'Everyone'.`,`The Supreme Court ruled 7 to 2 that games are protected as free speech (2011).`]},"tech:engine3d":{intro:`A true 3D engine builds everything from polygons in three dimensions: rooms, stairs, bridges, monsters and the rocket just fired. The player can look and move freely in every direction, and levels can stack rooms above rooms. Lighting is worked out in advance and stored with the walls, so lamps and shadows look rich at little cost.`,how:`Every frame the engine works out which parts of the level might be visible, transforms their polygons and draws them with texture and light combined. Light maps, small pictures of brightness computed when the level is built, are blended with the wall textures. Monsters are polygon models animated frame by frame. On a PC without a 3D card the main processor does it all, so its speed with decimal arithmetic matters.`,history:`In 1995 Parallax Software's Descent let players fly in any direction through 3D mine tunnels. In 1995 Michael Abrash left Microsoft to join John Carmack at id Software and speed up Quake's drawing code. In June 1996 id released Quake, with fully 3D levels, polygon monsters and precomputed light maps. Also in June 1996, Nintendo's Super Mario 64 opened a 3D castle with a camera the player could swing around. In 1996 Tomb Raider put Lara Croft in 3D tombs on the Saturn and PlayStation.`,facts:[`Quake needed a Pentium processor to run smoothly (1996).`,`Super Mario 64 sold about 11 million copies after its release in 1996.`]},"tech:analog":{intro:`An analog stick measures how far, not just which way, the player pushes it, so a character can tiptoe, walk or run with one thumb. A rumble motor makes the controller shake when the player crashes, fires or takes a hit. Together they make 3D worlds easier to move through and more physical to feel.`,how:`Inside, the stick turns two sensors, slotted wheels read by light beams or variable resistors, one for each axis, and the console reads their positions as numbers many times a second. A spring pulls the stick back to the centre. Rumble comes from a small electric motor spinning an off-centre weight, which shakes the whole controller.`,history:`In 1982 the Vectrex and the Atari 5200 had analogue joysticks, but most games of 1982 used simple on-off switches. In June 1996 the Nintendo 64 launched with a thumb stick in the middle of its three-handled controller, designed alongside Super Mario 64. In July 1996 Sega answered with an analogue pad for the Saturn. In April 1997 Nintendo's Rumble Pak plugged into the controller and shook in time with Star Fox 64. In November 1997 Sony's DualShock combined two sticks with two rumble motors. In 2004 a US jury ordered Sony to pay Immersion about $82 million for using its vibration patents.`,facts:[`The Nintendo 64 controller had three handles so it could be held two ways (1996).`,`The DualShock's two motors used weights of different sizes for strong and gentle shakes (1997).`]},"tech:gl3dgames":{intro:`A 3D-card game hands its triangles to a graphics accelerator instead of drawing every pixel with the main processor. The card fills polygons with smoothly filtered textures, so blocky pixels become clean surfaces, and higher resolutions run at faster frame rates. The same game suddenly looks sharp and moves smoothly.`,how:`The game sends corner points and textures to the card through a programming interface, and the card fills the triangles itself, adding filtering, transparency and fog. Many accelerators handle only 3D and sit beside the normal graphics card, linked to it by a short cable. Games must be rewritten or patched for each interface, and the best results need plenty of texture memory on the card.`,history:`In 1996 3dfx Interactive launched its Voodoo Graphics chip, and add-in boards such as Diamond's Monster 3D sold for about $299. In December 1996 Rendition released VQuake for its Vérité chip. In January 1997 id Software released GLQuake, which ran on the Voodoo through a small OpenGL driver. In 1997 patched versions of Tomb Raider showed how much better a 3D card made a game look. In August 1997 NVIDIA released the RIVA 128, which handled both 2D and 3D on one card. In February 1998 the Voodoo2 let two cards share the work of one picture.`,facts:[`The first Voodoo boards carried 4 MB of memory (1996).`,`GLQuake ran at 640 by 480 with filtered textures on a Voodoo (1997).`]},"tech:netcode":{intro:`Client-server netcode puts one computer, the server, in charge of the true state of the game, while each player's machine, the client, sends its inputs and shows what the server reports. To hide the delay of the connection, the client predicts its own player's movement and quietly corrects itself when the server disagrees.`,how:`The server runs the game in fixed steps, many times a second, and sends each client a snapshot of what it can see. The client moves its own player at once, as if the server had already agreed, and smooths other players between snapshots. When a shot is fired, the server can rewind to what the shooter saw on screen at that instant before deciding whether it hit.`,history:`In June 1996 Quake used a client-server design that played well on office networks but felt sluggish over home modems. In December 1996 John Carmack released QuakeWorld, which added client-side prediction so that a player's own movement felt instant. In 1998 Starsiege: Tribes sent each client only the most important updates to cope with modems. In 1998 Valve's Half-Life adopted similar methods, and in 2001 its engineer Yahn Bernier described lag compensation, which rewinds the server to check hits. In 2006 Tony Cannon's GGPO brought 'rollback' netcode to fighting games.`,facts:[`A dial-up modem often added more than 150 milliseconds of delay each way (1996).`,`QuakeWorld was a free download for Quake owners (1996).`]},"tech:mmo":{intro:`A massively multiplayer world is a persistent online game shared by thousands of players at once. It keeps running when you log off: other players hunt, trade and build while you sleep. Players pay a monthly subscription, and the world grows its own economy, politics and friendships.`,how:`No single computer can hold everyone, so the world runs on clusters of servers, split into regions and copied into parallel worlds, called shards, each with its own population. A database stores every character and item. The publisher's staff run the servers day and night, fix problems, add new areas and police cheating and abuse.`,history:`In 1986 Lucasfilm's Habitat ran a graphical online world for Commodore 64 owners on the Quantum Link service. In 1991 Neverwinter Nights on AOL let hundreds of players adventure together. In 1996 Meridian 59 offered a 3D world over the internet. In September 1997 Origin launched Ultima Online and explained its parallel servers as shards of a shattered crystal. In 1998 NCsoft launched Lineage in South Korea, where internet cafés made it a national craze. In March 1999 Sony's EverQuest followed, and in November 2004 Blizzard launched World of Warcraft.`,facts:[`Ultima Online cost $9.95 a month to play (1997).`,`World of Warcraft peaked at about 12 million subscribers in 2010.`]},"tech:phonegame":{intro:`A phone game comes built into a mobile phone, using its small black-and-white screen and number keys. With no download and nothing to buy, it reaches everyone who owns the phone. Simple ideas work best: steer a snake around the screen, eat the dots, grow longer and never bite your own tail.`,how:`The game is part of the phone's own software, sharing a small processor with the radio and the menus. The screen shows a few thousand black dots, the number keys serve as directions, and a game must be quick to pick up while waiting for a bus. Because it costs nothing extra, it is a selling point for the phone rather than a product of its own.`,history:`In 1976 Gremlin's arcade game Blockade introduced the idea behind Snake. In 1997 Nokia engineer Taneli Armanto put Snake on the Nokia 6110. In 2000 Snake II appeared on the Nokia 3310, one of the best-selling phones of its time. In 2001 NTT DoCoMo in Japan began selling downloadable Java games through its i-mode service. In 2003 Nokia's N-Gage, a phone shaped like a game handheld, sold poorly. In 2005 Electronic Arts bought the mobile games maker JAMDAT for $680 million.`,facts:[`Nokia sold about 126 million 3310 phones after its launch in 2000.`,`By 2005 Snake had shipped on hundreds of millions of Nokia phones.`]},"tech:engines":{intro:`A licensable game engine is the reusable machinery of a game, its renderer, physics, sound, networking and editing tools, sold to other studios. They fill it with their own levels, characters and story instead of building the technology from scratch. A studio can ship sooner, and the engine's makers earn from games they never designed.`,how:`The engine's makers keep the game-specific parts apart from the general machinery, and licensees receive source code, tools and support for a fee and often a share of sales. The editor lets artists build levels and see them as they will look in the game. Licensees change the code to suit their game, so each licensed game is a little different underneath.`,history:`In 1994 Raven Software built Heretic on id Software's Doom engine, and in 1995 Hexen followed. In 1996 Ken Silverman's Build engine powered 3D Realms' Duke Nukem 3D. In May 1998 Epic MegaGames released Unreal, with its UnrealEd level editor in the box, and began licensing the engine to other studios. In November 1998 Valve's Half-Life, built on a heavily modified Quake engine, won dozens of game-of-the-year awards. In 2001 Criterion's RenderWare engine helped Rockstar build Grand Theft Auto III. In 2005 Unity Technologies released the first version of its engine for the Mac.`,facts:[`In 1997 id Software released Doom's source code for anyone to study.`,`Half-Life won more than 50 game-of-the-year awards (1998).`]},"tech:texcomp":{intro:`Texture compression shrinks the pictures wrapped over 3D models so more of them fit in a graphics card's memory and reach its chips faster. Unlike a photograph format, it is designed so the chip can read any small block directly while drawing, with no unpacking first. Textures can be bigger and sharper in the same memory.`,how:`The picture is cut into blocks of 4 by 4 pixels. For each block the compressor stores two colours, and for each of the 16 pixels a two-bit choice between those colours and two blends of them: 8 bytes instead of 48. The chip decodes each block in hardware as it fetches it. Some detail is lost, especially at sharp edges and in smooth gradients, but most surfaces look the same.`,history:`In 1998 S3 introduced its S3TC compression with the Savage3D graphics chip. In 1998 Microsoft licensed it for DirectX 6, where its formats were called DXT1 to DXT5, and every major card maker followed. In 1998 Sega's Dreamcast used a different scheme, vector quantisation, in its PowerVR graphics chip. In 1999 RAD Game Tools released Bink, a compressed video format built for games. In 2009 DirectX 11 added newer formats for high-quality colour and lighting. In 2020 Sony's PlayStation 5 added hardware that unpacks Oodle Kraken data as it streams from the drive.`,facts:[`DXT1 stores each pixel in 4 bits, a sixth of normal 24-bit colour (1998).`,`ASTC, adopted in 2012, lets block sizes range from 4 by 4 to 12 by 12.`]},"tech:f2p":{intro:`Free-to-play games cost nothing to download and play. The money comes from selling optional extras: costumes, pets, faster progress or extra lives. Most players never pay, but a small share spend a great deal, and the game is designed to keep everyone playing for months so that some of them will.`,how:`The game is run as a service, not sold as a product: a shop inside it sells items for virtual currency bought with real money. Designers balance it so non-payers enjoy it while paying stays tempting, and they watch closely which items sell. Being free removes the barrier to trying it, so a game can reach millions; the risk is that players feel it is rigged towards spending.`,history:`In 1999 South Korea's Nexon launched QuizQuiz, an online quiz game that was free to play and sold optional items. In 2000 Finland's Sulake opened Habbo Hotel, selling virtual furniture for pixel-art rooms. In 2003 Nexon's MapleStory and in 2004 its KartRider drew millions of players in Korea. In 2009 Zynga's FarmVille on Facebook made selling virtual crops big business, and in October 2009 Riot Games launched League of Legends free of charge. In June 2011 Valve made Team Fortress 2 free and sold hats instead. In 2017 Fortnite Battle Royale arrived free on consoles and PCs.`,facts:[`FarmVille passed 80 million monthly players in 2010.`,`League of Legends had about 100 million monthly players in 2016.`]},"tech:physics":{intro:`A physics engine solves Newton's laws for the objects in a game many times a second, so crates tumble, barrels roll and bodies fall in a way that looks real. Instead of every reaction being animated by hand, the world responds to whatever the player does. Puzzles and fights can grow out of the physics itself.`,how:`Each object has a mass, a speed and a simple collision shape such as a box, sphere or capsule. Every frame the engine moves the objects, finds which shapes touch, and works out the pushes needed to stop them passing through each other. Joints link parts into rag dolls and vehicles. The hard part is keeping stacks steady and stopping fast objects from slipping through thin walls.`,history:`In 1998 Hugh Reynolds and Steven Collins founded Havok in Dublin, and its physics engine reached its first games in 2000. In 1998 DreamWorks' Trespasser had already tried full physics and became known for its bugs. In 2003 Max Payne 2 used Havok for tumbling rag-doll bodies. In November 2004 Valve's Half-Life 2 gave players a gravity gun to pick up and hurl objects. In 2006 Ageia sold a PhysX card, a separate processor just for physics. In 2007 Intel bought Havok, and in February 2008 NVIDIA bought Ageia. In 2015 Microsoft bought Havok from Intel.`,facts:[`Intel paid about $110 million for Havok in 2007.`,`Ageia's PhysX card cost about $300 in 2006, and few games used it.`]},"tech:esports":{intro:`Esports turns playing video games into a spectator sport. Professional players and teams compete in leagues and tournaments for prize money, in front of crowds in arenas and audiences watching on television or online. Sponsors, fan clubs and star players follow, as in football or motor racing.`,how:`Organisers book venues, set the rules and the game version, and pay prizes from sponsors, ticket sales and broadcast rights. Commentators explain the action as it happens, and replays show the key moments. Players train for hours every day, often living together in team houses. Games with deep strategy and fair, quick matches make the best sports.`,history:`In 1980 Atari's Space Invaders Championship drew about 10,000 players across the US. In 1997 Dennis Fong, known as Thresh, won John Carmack's Ferrari at the Red Annihilation Quake tournament. In 2000 South Korea's Korea e-Sports Association formed and cable channels began showing StarCraft matches, making top players celebrities. In June 2011 Twitch launched as a site for watching people play, and in 2014 Amazon bought it for $970 million. In 2021 Dota 2's International tournament had a prize pool of about $40 million, mostly paid for by fans.`,facts:[`In 2013 the US began granting athlete visas to professional League of Legends players.`,`Thresh's prize in 1997 was a red Ferrari 328.`]},"tech:shaderart":{intro:`Shader effects come from small programs that a graphics chip runs on every corner and every pixel it draws. Instead of a fixed menu of effects, artists and programmers write their own: rippling water that reflects the sky, metal that gleams, skin that glows softly, and shadows that move with the light.`,how:`A vertex shader moves and transforms each corner of a model; a pixel shader then works out the colour of each pixel from textures, lights and maths written by the game's programmers. Normal maps store tiny bumps as colours, so a flat surface lights as if finely detailed. Because the chip runs thousands of these little programs at once, effects that took film studios hours can run every frame.`,history:`In 1990 Pixar's RenderMan shading language let film artists write programs for the look of each surface. In November 2000 Microsoft's DirectX 8 added programmable vertex and pixel shaders, and in February 2001 NVIDIA's GeForce 3 ran them. In November 2001 the Xbox brought the same kind of chip to a console. In May 2002 The Elder Scrolls III: Morrowind showed off shimmering shader water. In December 2002 DirectX 9 added a high-level shading language that read like C. In 2004 Doom 3 lit every pixel in real time, and Half-Life 2 and Far Cry filled their worlds with shader effects.`,facts:[`NVIDIA's GeForce 3 had about 57 million transistors (2001).`,`The Xbox's NV2A graphics chip was a close cousin of the GeForce 3 (2001).`]},"tech:consolenet":{intro:`A console online service gives each player one account and one friends list for every game on the machine. Players find matches, talk through headsets while they play and download extra levels, all through the console maker's own network. In return they pay a yearly subscription.`,how:`The console maker runs central servers for sign-in, friends, matchmaking and voice chat, so each game does not need to build its own. Games group players into matches by skill and connection speed. The service also delivers patches and paid add-ons, and lets the console maker ban cheats and abusive players across every game at once.`,history:`In 1995 Nintendo's Satellaview sent games to Super Famicom owners in Japan by satellite broadcast. In 2000 Sega's Dreamcast came with a modem and the SegaNet service, and in December 2000 Phantasy Star Online let console players adventure together online. In November 2002 Microsoft launched Xbox Live for broadband only, with a starter kit and headset for $49.99. In July 2004 Xbox Live passed 1 million members, and in November 2004 Halo 2 made its matchmaking famous. In November 2006 Sony launched the free PlayStation Network. In April 2011 hackers broke into it, exposing 77 million accounts and shutting it down for over three weeks.`,facts:[`Xbox Live had about 250,000 members by early 2003.`,`The Xbox had a network socket built in but no modem (2001).`]},"tech:digidist":{intro:`Digital distribution sells games as downloads instead of boxes on a shop shelf. The player buys online, the game arrives over the internet, and a store program keeps it updated with patches. With no discs, printing or shops to pay for, more of the price can reach the people who made the game.`,how:`A client program on the player's computer holds a library of bought games, downloads them in pieces from servers around the world, and checks with the store that each copy is licensed. Updates arrive automatically, so every player has the same version. The store takes a share of each sale, typically about 30 per cent, and runs the prices, sales and featured games.`,history:`In 1994 the Sega Channel sent Mega Drive games to homes over cable television. In September 2003 Valve launched Steam to deliver updates for its own games. In November 2004 Half-Life 2 required Steam to activate, and many players grumbled. Also in November 2004, Xbox Live Arcade began selling small downloadable games on the Xbox. In 2005 Steam began selling games by other companies. In 2012 Steam Greenlight let players vote on which independent games it would sell. In December 2018 Epic Games opened a rival store that took only 12 per cent of each sale.`,facts:[`Rag Doll Kung Fu was the first game from another company sold on Steam (2005).`,`Epic's store kept 12 per cent of each sale against Steam's usual 30 per cent (2018).`]},"tech:touchgame":{intro:`Touchscreen games are played by tapping, dragging, drawing and swiping directly on the picture. There are no buttons to learn, so people who never played games, from grandparents to toddlers, can join in. New kinds of game appear: drawing paths, flicking objects and tracing shapes with a fingertip or stylus.`,how:`A resistive screen has two thin layers that touch where pressed and reads one point precisely, usually under a plastic stylus. A capacitive screen senses the electric charge of fingers through glass and can follow several at once. The game turns touches into actions, and designers must keep the player's hand from hiding what matters on screen.`,history:`In November 2004 Nintendo launched the DS, a two-screen handheld whose lower screen was touch-sensitive. In 2005 Nintendogs and Brain Training on the DS won over millions of people who had never owned a console. In June 2007 Apple's iPhone brought a multi-touch screen worked with bare fingers. In December 2009 Rovio's Angry Birds turned flinging birds with a fingertip into a worldwide hit. In 2010 Fruit Ninja and Cut the Rope followed it up the charts.`,facts:[`The Nintendo DS family sold about 154 million units after its launch in 2004.`,`Angry Birds passed 1 billion downloads in 2012.`]},"tech:unifiedgpu":{intro:`A unified shader GPU stops building separate units for the corners of shapes and for the pixels that fill them. Instead it has one kind of small programmable core, a great many of them, and hands each whatever work is waiting. No part of the chip sits idle while another part is overloaded.`,how:`Older chips had a fixed number of vertex units and pixel units: a scene of countless tiny triangles left the pixel units waiting, and a scene of big, richly shaded surfaces did the opposite. A unified chip keeps a pool of identical cores and a scheduler that hands them batches of whichever job is queued. The same design makes the chip simpler to program and to scale up.`,history:`In November 2005 Microsoft's Xbox 360 launched with ATI's Xenos graphics chip, the first with unified shaders, holding 48 shader units and 10 MB of fast on-chip memory. In November 2006 NVIDIA's GeForce 8800 brought unified shaders to PCs with 128 cores. In January 2007 Windows Vista arrived with DirectX 10, built around a single shader model for every stage. In February 2007 NVIDIA released CUDA, which let programmers use the GPU's cores for general calculations in a language like C. In 2012 researchers in Toronto trained the AlexNet image-recognition network on two NVIDIA gaming cards.`,facts:[`The GeForce 8800 GTX had 681 million transistors (2006).`,`AlexNet was trained on two GeForce GTX 580 cards (2012).`]},"tech:ugc":{intro:`A player-made game platform is a free game that is really a toolkit and a shop. Children and hobbyists build their own small games inside it with simple tools, publish them instantly to every other player, and earn when people spend the platform's currency in their creations. The platform keeps a share of every coin.`,how:`The platform supplies a world engine, ready-made physics and characters, an editor and a simple scripting language. Creations run on the platform's servers and appear in a shared catalogue, so players hop from one game to another without installing anything. Popularity charts decide what gets seen. Running it means checking millions of creations for safety, because many players are children.`,history:`In 2003 Linden Lab opened Second Life, where residents built and sold virtual goods for Linden dollars. In September 2006 David Baszucki and Erik Cassel launched Roblox, with games scripted in the Lua language. In October 2008 Sony's LittleBigPlanet promised 'Play, Create, Share' on the PlayStation 3. In 2009 Minecraft appeared, and its players began building worlds and servers of their own. In 2013 Roblox began letting creators swap its Robux currency for real money. In March 2021 Roblox listed its shares on the New York Stock Exchange, and in March 2023 Epic released an editor for building games inside Fortnite.`,facts:[`Roblox was valued at about $45 billion on its first day of trading (2021).`,`Roblox had more than 30 million daily users by 2020.`]},"tech:motion":{intro:`Motion controls read how the player moves, so swinging the arm swings a bat and tilting the hands steers a car. A small accelerometer in the controller feels movement, and a camera tracks where it points at the screen. Games become easy for anyone to understand, and whole families play together in the living room.`,how:`An accelerometer is a microscopic silicon spring and weight that measures pushes in three directions, including the pull of gravity, which shows how the controller is tilted. To point, a tiny infrared camera in the controller sees two clusters of infrared lights in a bar by the television and works out where on the screen it is aimed. A wireless link sends the readings to the console many times a second.`,history:`In 1989 Mattel's Power Glove for the NES promised gesture control but worked poorly. In 2003 Sony's EyeToy camera let PlayStation 2 players wave their arms at the screen. In November 2006 Nintendo's Wii launched with the Wii Remote and Wii Sports, and in December 2006 Nintendo replaced millions of wrist straps after remotes flew into television sets. In 2009 Wii MotionPlus added a gyroscope for finer tracking. In 2010 Sony launched PlayStation Move, and in November 2010 Microsoft's Kinect tracked whole bodies with a depth camera and no controller at all. In 2017 Microsoft stopped making Kinect.`,facts:[`The Wii sold about 101 million consoles after its launch in 2006.`,`Wii Sports sold about 83 million copies, most bundled with the console (2006).`,`Kinect sold 8 million units in its first 60 days (2010).`]},"tech:appgames":{intro:`An app store is a single shop built into every phone, open to any developer who pays a small yearly fee. Games are bought with a tap, downloaded over the air and charged to an account. A tiny team can reach millions of players without a publisher, a box or a shop.`,how:`Developers submit their games to the store's owner, which reviews them, hosts the downloads, takes the payments and keeps a share of each sale, typically 30 per cent. Charts and featured lists decide which games are seen, so a place near the top can make a studio's fortune. Prices fall fast, and many games sell for less than a dollar.`,history:`In July 2008 Apple opened its App Store with about 500 apps, letting developers keep 70 per cent of each sale. In 2008 Sega's Super Monkey Ball, at $9.99, was among its first big games. In October 2008 Google opened the Android Market for Android phones. In December 2009 Rovio's Angry Birds, priced at 99 cents, became one of the store's biggest hits. In August 2020 Epic Games' Fortnite was removed from Apple's store after Epic added its own payment system. In November 2020 Apple announced it would cut its share to 15 per cent for small developers.`,facts:[`The App Store recorded 10 million downloads in its first weekend (2008).`,`Apple's developer programme cost $99 a year (2008).`]},"tech:iap":{intro:`In-app purchases let a player buy things inside a game: extra lives, new levels, virtual coins or decorations. Live operations means running the game like a shop that never closes, changing it every week, measuring what players do and testing which offers they respond to. The game earns for as long as people keep playing.`,how:`Every tap and purchase is logged and sent to the developer's servers, so the team can see where players get stuck or give up. In an A/B test, two groups of players see different prices, rewards or difficulty, and whichever version earns more or keeps players longer wins. Events, limited-time offers and new content arrive on a schedule to bring players back.`,history:`In June 2009 Zynga launched FarmVille on Facebook, selling farm animals and decorations for real money. In October 2009 Apple began allowing in-app purchases inside free iPhone apps. In August 2012 Supercell released Clash of Clans, and in November 2012 King brought Candy Crush Saga to phones. In January 2014 Apple agreed with the US Federal Trade Commission to refund parents for purchases their children had made without permission. In 2016 Activision Blizzard bought King for $5.9 billion, and Tencent bought most of Supercell.`,facts:[`Apple's 2014 settlement promised at least $32.5 million in refunds.`,`Tencent's 2016 deal valued Supercell at about $10 billion.`]},"tech:freeengine":{intro:`Free engines put professional 3D game tools, once licensed for huge sums, into anyone's hands at no upfront cost. Students, hobbyists and tiny studios get the same renderer, physics and editors as large companies. The engine maker earns only if a game succeeds, through a share of its revenue or paid upgrades.`,how:`The engine is downloaded with its editor, manuals and example projects, and an online store sells ready-made models, sounds and code. Licences are free below a revenue threshold, with a royalty or a paid edition above it. A huge community answers questions and makes tutorials, and the same project can be exported to many platforms at once.`,history:`In June 2005 Unity released its first engine, for the Mac. In October 2009 Unity made its basic edition free, and in November 2009 Epic released the Unreal Development Kit free for non-commercial use. In 2010 Unity opened its Asset Store for buying ready-made parts. In February 2014 the Godot engine was released as open-source software, free for any use. In March 2015 Epic made Unreal Engine 4 free with a 5 per cent royalty on larger revenues, and Unity 5's Personal edition became free the same month. In September 2023 Unity's plan to charge per installation caused an uproar, and in September 2024 it was scrapped.`,facts:[`Unreal Engine 4 had cost $19 a month before it became free (2014).`,`Godot is released under the permissive MIT licence (2014).`]},"tech:cloudgame":{intro:`Cloud gaming runs the game on a powerful server in a distant data centre and streams the picture to the player as video. The player's device sends back the button presses. Any cheap screen with a good connection can play the newest games, but every millisecond of delay between pressing and seeing shows.`,how:`The server draws each frame, compresses it as video in a few milliseconds and sends it over the internet; the player's device decodes it and returns the controller inputs. The total delay must stay low enough to feel responsive, so servers must sit within a few hundred kilometres of players. Light in fibre covers about 200 kilometres a millisecond, and every router on the way adds more. A steady connection matters as much as a fast one.`,history:`In March 2009 Steve Perlman unveiled OnLive at the Game Developers Conference, and in June 2010 it launched in the US. In August 2012 OnLive ran out of money and was sold to new owners. In July 2012 Sony bought the streaming company Gaikai, and in 2014 it launched PlayStation Now. In April 2015 Sony bought OnLive's patents and the service shut down. In November 2019 Google launched Stadia, and in January 2023 it closed the service and refunded players. In 2020 NVIDIA's GeForce Now and Microsoft's Xbox Cloud Gaming opened to the public.`,facts:[`Sony paid $380 million for Gaikai in 2012.`,`Google refunded all Stadia hardware and game purchases when it closed in 2023.`]},"tech:vr":{intro:`A consumer VR headset puts a screen close to each eye, behind lenses, and tracks the head so precisely that turning to look makes the virtual world turn with you. Done well, the brain accepts the place as real. Done badly, with lag or blur, it makes people sick within minutes.`,how:`Phone-style displays show a slightly different image to each eye for depth, and lenses spread them across a wide field of view. Gyroscopes and accelerometers sense head movement hundreds of times a second, helped by cameras or infrared beacons that fix the headset's position in the room. The whole chain from movement to new picture must take under about 20 milliseconds, so the computer draws 90 frames a second or more.`,history:`In 1968 Ivan Sutherland built a head-mounted display, nicknamed the Sword of Damocles, that hung from the ceiling. In 1995 Nintendo's Virtual Boy flopped, giving players headaches with its red-only images. In August 2012 Palmer Luckey's Oculus Rift raised about $2.4 million on Kickstarter, and in 2013 John Carmack joined Oculus. In March 2014 Facebook bought Oculus for about $2 billion. In 2016 the Rift launched at $599, HTC and Valve's Vive at $799 and Sony's PlayStation VR at $399. In March 2020 Half-Life: Alyx gave VR its biggest game, and in October 2020 the standalone Quest 2 sold for $299.`,facts:[`Nintendo sold only about 770,000 Virtual Boys (1995).`,`The Oculus Kickstarter aimed for $250,000 and raised nearly ten times as much (2012).`]},"tech:arloc":{intro:`Location-based augmented reality games turn the real map of your town into the game board. The phone's satellite positioning knows where you are, and landmarks become places to visit, fight over or collect from. The camera can show creatures standing in the street. Players walk for miles and meet strangers playing the same game.`,how:`The game combines satellite positioning, mobile data and a database of real places, many suggested by players: statues, churches, murals and post offices. Servers place game objects near those spots and check each player's position. The camera view is optional; the real magic is getting people outside. Battery drain, safety and crowds at popular spots are the problems.`,history:`In May 2000 the US stopped deliberately blurring civilian GPS signals, and that month the first geocache was hidden near Portland, Oregon. In November 2012 John Hanke's Niantic Labs, inside Google, launched Ingress, in which two teams fought over real-world 'portals'. In October 2015 Niantic became an independent company. In July 2016 Niantic released Pokémon GO with Nintendo and The Pokémon Company, turning Ingress's portals into PokéStops and gyms. In the summer of 2016 crowds of players filled parks in cities around the world. In 2025 Niantic sold its games business, including Pokémon GO, to Scopely for $3.5 billion.`,facts:[`Pokémon GO was downloaded about 500 million times in 2016.`,`Pokémon GO earned about $1 billion in its first seven months (2017).`]},"tech:battlepass":{intro:`A battle pass is a ticket for one season of a game, bought for about the price of a cinema ticket. Playing earns points that unlock a long ladder of rewards, mostly costumes and decorations, before the season ends. Players know exactly what they will get, and the deadline keeps them coming back.`,how:`Each season lasts a few months and has its own theme, story and reward track of dozens of tiers. A free track gives some rewards to everyone; the paid pass unlocks the rest and often returns enough virtual currency to buy the next one. Daily and weekly challenges give reasons to log in, and a new season resets the ladder.`,history:`In 2013 Valve sold a digital Compendium for Dota 2's International tournament, putting a quarter of its sales into the prize pool. In December 2017 Fortnite Battle Royale's second season introduced a Battle Pass for 950 V-Bucks, about $9.50. In February 2018 Fortnite's third season stretched the pass to 100 tiers. In February 2019 Apex Legends launched free, and in March 2019 it added a pass of its own. By 2019 battle passes had spread to most big online games, replacing many paid loot boxes.`,facts:[`Fortnite earned about $2.4 billion in 2018, a record for one game in one year.`,`Compendium sales helped lift Dota 2's 2013 prize pool to about $2.9 million.`]},"tech:healthyplay":{intro:`Spending and play-time limits are rules, set by governments or game companies, that protect players, especially children, from overspending and from playing too much. Loot boxes, paid random prizes, look like gambling to many regulators. Some countries ban them, and others cap how long minors may play.`,how:`Regulators decide whether paid random rewards count as gambling, which can force games to remove them, publish the odds or block them for young players. Play-time rules rely on real-name registration and identity checks, so a game can lock children out outside permitted hours. Consoles and phones add parental controls for spending and screen time.`,history:`In 2011 South Korea passed a 'shutdown law' blocking online games for under-16s between midnight and 6 am. In November 2017 a row over paid loot boxes in Star Wars Battlefront II made headlines worldwide. In April 2018 Belgium's Gaming Commission ruled that paid loot boxes in several games were illegal gambling. In 2019 China limited minors to 90 minutes of online games on weekdays, and in May 2019 the World Health Organization recognised gaming disorder as a condition. In August 2021 China cut minors' online play to one hour on Fridays, weekends and holidays. In 2021 South Korea voted to scrap its shutdown law.`,facts:[`China's 2021 rules let minors play online only from 8 to 9 pm on permitted days.`,`Apple required games to show loot box odds from December 2017.`]},"tech:rtx":{intro:`Real-time ray tracing follows rays of light from the eye into a scene and bounces them off mirrors, glass and walls to find what each pixel should show. Reflections, soft shadows and light spilling between surfaces come out right without artists faking them. Film studios spent hours on each frame; games want sixty a second.`,how:`Special cores on the graphics chip test rays against the scene's triangles, which are sorted into a tree of nested boxes so most can be skipped at once. Games can afford only a ray or two per pixel, so the raw result is grainy, and a denoising filter smooths it. Ordinary rasterised drawing does most of the work, with rays added for reflections, shadows and lighting.`,history:`In 1968 Arthur Appel at IBM cast rays to work out shadows for computer drawings. In 1979 Turner Whitted at Bell Labs showed recursive ray tracing of reflections and refractions, with a single picture taking more than an hour to compute. In 2006 Pixar's Cars was the studio's first film to use ray tracing widely. In March 2018 Microsoft announced DirectX Raytracing, and in September 2018 NVIDIA's RTX 20 cards added dedicated ray-tracing cores. In November 2018 Battlefield V became the first game to use them. In November 2020 the PlayStation 5 and Xbox Series X brought ray-tracing hardware to consoles.`,facts:[`NVIDIA's RTX 2080 Ti cost $1,199 at launch (2018).`,`AMD added ray-tracing hardware to its Radeon RX 6000 graphics cards in 2020.`]},"tech:dlss":{intro:`Neural upscaling draws a game at a lower resolution and lets a trained neural network fill in the missing detail, producing a sharp, larger picture. The graphics chip does less work for each frame, so games run faster or can afford heavier effects. The network has learned from very high-quality images what fine detail should look like.`,how:`Each frame is drawn at, say, half the width and height, along with motion vectors that record how each pixel moved since the last frame. The network combines the new frame with earlier ones to rebuild detail, running on the chip's matrix-maths units. Frame generation goes further and invents whole in-between frames. Mistakes show as smearing or ghosting around fast movement.`,history:`In September 2018 NVIDIA announced Deep Learning Super Sampling with its RTX 20 cards, and in February 2019 it appeared in Battlefield V and Metro Exodus. In 2019 reviewers found the first version blurry. In March 2020 DLSS 2.0 used one general network fed with motion data from earlier frames, and in many games the results matched native resolution. In June 2021 AMD answered with FidelityFX Super Resolution, which used no neural network. In October 2022 DLSS 3 added frame generation with the RTX 40 cards. In January 2025 DLSS 4 could generate up to three frames for every one drawn.`,facts:[`The first DLSS needed a separate network trained for each game (2019).`,`Intel launched its own neural upscaler, XeSS, in 2022.`]},"tech:neuralgame":{intro:`A neural game engine has no code for rules and no designed levels. A neural network, trained on many hours of recorded play, predicts each new frame from the previous frames and the player's key presses. The game is, in effect, a dream of a game that the network keeps consistent from moment to moment.`,how:`Like image generators, the model is a diffusion network that turns noise into a picture, guided by recent frames and actions. It learns physics, collisions and scores only as patterns in pixels, so it can forget what was behind the player or let a defeated enemy return. Every frame needs powerful AI chips, and keeping a world consistent for more than a few minutes is the hardest part.`,history:`In 2018 David Ha and Jürgen Schmidhuber showed 'world models' that learned to dream simple Doom and car-racing levels. In May 2020 NVIDIA's GameGAN recreated Pac-Man after watching 50,000 games of it. In August 2024 Google researchers' GameNGen ran a playable Doom at about 20 frames a second on a single TPU chip. In October 2024 Decart and Etched released Oasis, a Minecraft-like world generated frame by frame. In December 2024 Google DeepMind showed Genie 2, and in August 2025 Genie 3 generated explorable worlds from a text prompt in real time, staying consistent for minutes.`,facts:[`Testers told short GameNGen clips from real Doom only slightly better than chance (2024).`,`Genie 3 generated worlds at 24 frames a second in 720p (2025).`]},"tech:ainpc":{intro:`AI characters give the people in a game a small language model, a memory and a synthetic voice, so they can hold real conversations instead of repeating a few written lines. Players talk to them by typing or speaking, and the characters answer in character, remember what happened and react to events in the world.`,how:`Each character is defined by a written description of its personality, knowledge and goals, plus rules about what it must never say. Speech recognition turns the player's voice into text, the language model writes a reply, and a speech engine voices it with matching lip movement. Small models running on the player's own graphics chip cut delay and cost, while guardrails stop characters breaking the story.`,history:`In 1966 Joseph Weizenbaum's ELIZA chatted by turning users' words back into questions. In 2005 Michael Mateas and Andrew Stern's Façade let players type freely to a quarrelling couple. In April 2023 Stanford researchers filled a small virtual town with 25 language-model characters, who organised a Valentine's Day party among themselves. In May 2023 NVIDIA showed its ACE technology with a ramen-shop owner who answered spoken questions. In March 2025 Krafton's life simulation inZOI used on-device AI for its simulated people. In May 2025 Fortnite added a talking Darth Vader using an AI copy of James Earl Jones's voice, and the actors' union SAG-AFTRA complained.`,facts:[`In 2024 US video game actors began a strike over AI that lasted almost a year.`,`Each of Stanford's 25 characters kept a written memory of what it saw and did (2023).`]},"tech:coaxcarrier":{intro:`A coaxial carrier system sends hundreds of telephone calls down a single copper tube at once. Each call is shifted to its own frequency, like stations on a radio dial, and amplifiers every few kilometres boost the fading signals. One cable between cities can do the work of thousands of pairs of wires.`,how:`A coaxial line is a copper tube with a single wire held at its centre by insulating discs, which stops high frequencies leaking away. At the sending end each voice is moved up into its own slot about 4 kilohertz wide, and groups of slots are stacked into ever bigger blocks. Repeater amplifiers along the route make up for losses, and at the far end filters sort the calls back out.`,history:`In 1929 Lloyd Espenschied and Herman Affel at AT&T applied for a patent on coaxial cable for carrying many messages at once. In 1936 AT&T tested a coaxial line between New York and Philadelphia. In 1941 the first commercial L1 system opened between Minneapolis and Stevens Point, Wisconsin, carrying 480 calls. In 1953 the L3 system carried 1,860 calls on each pair of tubes, or a television channel alongside 600 calls. In 1974 the L5 system carried 10,800 calls on a single tube.`,facts:[`L3 repeaters stood about every 6 kilometres along the cable (1953).`,`The first L1 route was about 320 kilometres long (1941).`]},"tech:microwave":{intro:`Microwave relay carries telephone calls and television across country on narrow radio beams passed from tower to tower. Each hilltop station catches the beam, amplifies it and sends it on to the next, about 50 kilometres away. No cable has to be laid across the land, only a clear line of sight between towers.`,how:`Microwaves, radio waves just a few centimetres long, can be focused by dish or horn antennas into tight beams that carry a wide band of frequencies, enough for hundreds of calls or a television picture. The curve of the Earth and the hills limit each hop to the distance one tower can see. Heavy rain weakens the signal, so routes are planned with a safety margin.`,history:`In 1947 AT&T opened an experimental microwave link between New York and Boston with seven relay towers. On 17 August 1951 AT&T's TD-2 system carried its first coast-to-coast telephone call. On 4 September 1951 the same route carried the first coast-to-coast live television broadcast, President Truman's speech at the Japanese peace conference in San Francisco. In 1963 Jack Goeken founded Microwave Communications Inc, or MCI, to build microwave links for businesses. In 1969 the Federal Communications Commission approved MCI's route between Chicago and St Louis, an early crack in AT&T's monopoly on long distance.`,facts:[`The 1951 coast-to-coast route used about 100 relay towers.`,`The relay towers on the 1951 route stood roughly 48 kilometres apart.`]},"tech:subcoax":{intro:`An undersea telephone cable lays a copper line along the ocean floor between continents, with amplifiers sealed inside it at regular intervals. Dozens of people can talk across an ocean at once, clearly and in any weather. The amplifiers are built to work for decades without ever being visited.`,how:`Two cables run side by side, one for each direction. Every 69 kilometres a flexible repeater, a long armoured tube holding vacuum-tube amplifiers, is spliced into the line and laid with it from a ship. Power is fed along the cable's own conductor from the shore. Every part is tested for extreme reliability, because raising a repeater from four kilometres down costs a fortune.`,history:`In 1858 the first transatlantic telegraph cable carried messages for only a few weeks before failing. In 1927 a radio telephone service opened between London and New York, but it was costly and faded in bad weather. On 25 September 1956 TAT-1 opened between Gallanach Bay in Scotland and Clarenville in Newfoundland, built by Britain's Post Office, AT&T and Canada's overseas telecommunications company. In its first 24 hours of service in 1956, TAT-1 carried 588 calls between London and the US. In 1959 TAT-2 linked France with Newfoundland. In 1978 TAT-1 was retired.`,facts:[`A three-minute radio call from New York to London cost $75 in 1927.`,`TAT-1 opened with 36 telephone circuits (1956).`]},"tech:tasi":{intro:`Speech interpolation squeezes more conversations into scarce channels by using the silences in speech. People pause, listen and breathe for more than half of every call. A fast switch notices who is talking and lends each idle channel to someone who is, switching it back within milliseconds.`,how:`Detectors listen to every conversation. When a talker starts, the system grabs a free channel and sends a short signal telling the far end which call it now carries, quickly enough that the first syllable is barely clipped. Because only a minority of talkers are speaking at any instant, many more calls than channels can share a link. When too many talk at once, a little speech is lost.`,history:`In the 1950s Bell Labs engineers measured that each direction of a telephone call is silent more than half the time. In June 1960 their Time Assignment Speech Interpolation system, TASI, went into service on the TAT-1 transatlantic cable and nearly doubled the conversations it could carry. In the 1980s digital speech interpolation did the same job on digital satellite and cable links. In 1991 GSM mobile phones used a related trick, sending almost nothing while their user was silent to save battery and reduce interference.`,facts:[`TASI let TAT-1's 37 working channels carry 72 conversations (1960).`,`TASI worked best on busy links with many calls, where the pauses averaged out (1960).`]},"tech:modem":{intro:`A modem, short for modulator-demodulator, turns a computer's ones and zeros into tones that an ordinary telephone line can carry, and turns them back at the other end. Computers and terminals in different cities can exchange data over the existing phone network instead of special lines.`,how:`The simplest modems use two pitches, one for a one and one for a zero, a method called frequency-shift keying; a different pair of pitches in each direction lets both ends send at once. Speed is limited by the narrow band of frequencies a voice line passes. Modems are rented from the telephone company and wired into the line, while acoustic couplers clamp onto an ordinary handset instead.`,history:`In 1958 AT&T's Bell 101 linked radar sites for the US Air Force's SAGE air-defence network at 110 bits a second. In 1962 the Bell 103 sent data at 300 bits a second in both directions at once over ordinary dial-up lines. In 1968 the Carterfone decision allowed Americans to connect non-Bell equipment to the phone network, and independent modem makers followed. By 1970 timesharing customers were dialling into distant computers from Teletype terminals through acoustic couplers.`,facts:[`The Bell 103's 300 bits a second meant about 30 characters a second (1962).`,`The 1968 Carterfone case was named after a radio-to-telephone link made by Thomas Carter.`]},"tech:tcarrier":{intro:`Digital carrier measures each telephone voice thousands of times a second, turns each measurement into a number and sends the numbers as pulses. Because a pulse only has to be recognised as on or off, repeaters along the line can rebuild it perfectly, so noise no longer piles up with distance. One pair of wires carries 24 calls.`,how:`Each voice is sampled 8,000 times a second and each sample coded as an 8-bit number, a method called pulse-code modulation. Samples from 24 calls are interleaved into a frame, with one extra bit to mark where it starts: 193 bits, 8,000 times a second, gives 1.544 megabits a second. Regenerating repeaters sit about every 1.8 kilometres, in the manholes where older cables already had loading coils.`,history:`In 1937 Alec Reeves, a British engineer working in Paris, invented pulse-code modulation, but the valves of the time made it too costly to use. In 1947 the transistor, invented at Bell Labs, promised small, cheap circuits that could code and decode it. In 1962 the Bell System put its first T1 lines into service, carrying calls between telephone exchanges over existing copper pairs. In the 1970s T1 spread through city networks, because it was cheaper than laying new cables. In the 1990s many businesses rented T1 lines for their internet connections.`,facts:[`A T1 frame holds 193 bits and repeats 8,000 times a second (1962).`,`Each T1 call used 64 kilobits a second (1962).`]},"tech:ess":{intro:`Electronic switching puts a stored-program computer in charge of connecting telephone calls. Instead of thousands of relays clattering through fixed wiring, a program decides how to route each call, and new services can be added by changing the software. Exchanges become faster, smaller and easier to change.`,how:`Scanners check every line many times a second for a lifted receiver. The central processor, duplicated so one copy can take over if the other fails, collects the dialled digits, finds a free path and orders a network of small magnetically latched switches, called ferreeds, to connect the call. The program sits in a memory that cannot be erased by accident, while a separate memory tracks the calls in progress.`,history:`In November 1960 Bell Labs opened a trial electronic exchange in Morris, Illinois. In 1963 the Bell System introduced Touch-Tone push-button telephones, whose tones an electronic exchange could read quickly. In May 1965 the first No 1 ESS went into service in Succasunna, New Jersey, after years of development. In 1965 it offered features such as speed dialling and call forwarding that relay exchanges could not easily provide. In the 1970s No 1 ESS exchanges spread through American cities, each serving tens of thousands of lines.`,facts:[`No 1 ESS was designed to be out of service no more than two hours in 40 years (1965).`,`Touch-Tone phones first went on sale in Pennsylvania in 1963.`]},"tech:digitalswitch":{intro:`Digital switching keeps calls as streams of numbers inside the exchange, switching them by moving samples between time slots rather than by closing metal contacts. Joined to digital carrier lines, a call can stay digital from one end of the network to the other, with no extra noise added at each switch.`,how:`Each incoming digital line delivers samples from many calls in a repeating cycle of time slots. The switch writes each sample into memory and reads it out into a different slot on a different outgoing line, so connecting a call only means changing a table. A computer controls it all. With no moving parts, the switch is smaller, cheaper to run and easy to give new features.`,history:`In 1970 France's telephone research centre put an early E10, the first time-division digital exchange in public service, to work near Lannion in Brittany. In January 1976 AT&T opened its first No 4 ESS in Chicago, a digital long-distance switch handling tens of thousands of circuits. In 1979 Northern Telecom's DMS-100 became one of the first digital switches for local telephone lines in North America. In 1982 AT&T put its No 5 ESS for local exchanges into service in Illinois. By the 1990s most telephone exchanges in rich countries were digital.`,facts:[`Time-slot switching works in cycles of 125 microseconds, one sample per call (1976).`,`The No 4 ESS connected exchanges to each other, not homes (1976).`]},"tech:packetsw":{intro:`Packet switching chops each message into small blocks, packets, that travel through a network independently and are put back together at the destination. No line sits reserved and idle for one conversation, as it does in a telephone call; links are shared by everyone's packets. If a node fails, packets simply take another route.`,how:`Each packet carries a header with its destination and its place in the message. Nodes store each incoming packet briefly, check it for errors and forward it along whichever link looks best, so the network adapts to traffic and failures. Computer traffic, mostly silence with sudden bursts, suits this far better than circuits held open for minutes. The price is that packets may be delayed, lost or arrive out of order.`,history:`In 1961 Leonard Kleinrock at MIT began studying the mathematics of message queues in data networks. In 1964 Paul Baran at the RAND Corporation published 'On Distributed Communications', a set of reports on a network that could survive nuclear attack by sending message blocks over many paths. In 1965 Donald Davies at Britain's National Physical Laboratory reached the same idea independently, and in 1966 he named the blocks 'packets'. In 1967 his colleague Roger Scantlebury presented the NPL design at a conference in Gatlinburg, Tennessee, where ARPA's planners heard it. In 1969 the ARPANET was built on packet switching, and in 1970 the NPL's own network went into use.`,facts:[`Baran's design split messages into blocks of 1,024 bits (1964).`,`Davies chose the word 'packet' partly because it translated easily into other languages (1966).`]},"tech:arpanet":{intro:`The ARPANET links research computers at American universities and laboratories by packet switching. Each big host computer is joined to a small dedicated computer, an Interface Message Processor, which handles the networking and passes packets to the others over leased telephone lines. Researchers can log in to distant machines and share programs and files.`,how:`The IMPs, rugged minicomputers built by the firm BBN, break messages into packets, keep tables of the quickest routes and forward packets along 50-kilobit lines rented from AT&T. Each host only has to talk to its own IMP. Protocols written up as numbered Requests for Comments let different makes of computer understand one another.`,history:`In 1966 Bob Taylor at the US Defense Department's research agency, ARPA, won funding for a network to share its costly research computers, and Larry Roberts led the design. In January 1969 BBN in Cambridge, Massachusetts, began building the IMPs, and in April 1969 graduate student Steve Crocker wrote Request for Comments number 1. On 29 October 1969 Charley Kline at UCLA tried to log in to a computer at SRI, and the system crashed after the letters 'LO'. By December 1969 four sites were linked: UCLA, SRI, UC Santa Barbara and the University of Utah. In 1971 Ray Tomlinson sent email between machines using the @ sign. In 1973 the network reached Norway and London.`,facts:[`Each IMP was a Honeywell DDP-516 with 12,288 words of memory (1969).`,`The ARPANET had 213 host computers in 1981.`]},"tech:tcpip":{intro:`TCP/IP is a way of sending data across many different networks joined together. Every packet travels in a standard envelope with an address that any network can read, and the computers at each end number the packets, check them and resend whatever goes missing. The networks in between need not be reliable, or even alike.`,how:`Gateways between networks read only the envelope and pass packets on, without caring what is inside or whether each one arrives, so they can be simple and the networks can differ wildly. All the hard work of reliable delivery happens in the end computers: the receiver acknowledges what it gets, and the sender repeats anything not acknowledged in time. A sliding window lets many packets be on their way at once.`,history:`In 1973 Vint Cerf of Stanford and Bob Kahn of ARPA began designing a way to join the ARPANET to radio and satellite packet networks. In May 1974 they published 'A Protocol for Packet Network Intercommunication' in an IEEE journal, and in December 1974 RFC 675 first used the word 'internet'. In November 1977 a test sent packets from a van in California through radio, satellite and ARPANET links via Norway and London and back. In 1978 the design was split into TCP and a simpler IP underneath. In September 1981 the standards were published as RFC 791 and RFC 793. On 1 January 1983 the ARPANET switched over to TCP/IP.`,facts:[`An IP address of 1981 was 32 bits long, enough for about 4.3 billion addresses.`,`RFC 675 of 1974 used 'internet' as short for internetworking.`]},"tech:ethernet":{intro:`Ethernet joins the computers in a building on one shared cable. Any machine can send at any time; if two start together, both notice the collision, wait a random moment and try again. It is simple, cheap and fast enough for printers, file servers and electronic mail inside an office.`,how:`Each computer listens before sending, then transmits a frame carrying the addresses of sender and receiver; every other machine hears it and keeps only frames addressed to itself. If the signal on the cable is garbled, both senders stop and each waits a random time, doubling the range after every new collision, so even a busy network sorts itself out. Each machine on the cable has its own address.`,history:`On 22 May 1973 Bob Metcalfe at Xerox's Palo Alto Research Center wrote a memo describing Ethernet, inspired by the ALOHAnet radio network in Hawaii. In 1973 he and David Boggs had it running at 2.94 megabits a second, linking Xerox Alto computers and laser printers. In July 1976 Metcalfe and Boggs published their paper describing it. In 1979 Metcalfe left Xerox and founded 3Com. In 1980 Digital Equipment, Intel and Xerox published a 10-megabit standard, and in 1983 the IEEE adopted it as 802.3. In 1990 10BASE-T ran Ethernet over cheap telephone-style twisted-pair cable.`,facts:[`The 1980 standard's thick yellow cable could run 500 metres per segment.`,`Ethernet's name came from the 'luminiferous ether' once thought to carry light (1973).`]},"tech:internet":{intro:`The Internet is a network of networks: separate networks run by different organisations, joined by gateways and all speaking TCP/IP. A computer on any one of them can reach a computer on any other. On a single agreed day the ARPANET drops its old protocol, and every connected machine must speak the new one.`,how:`Each network keeps its own technology and management, and gateways pass packets between them. Every host has a numeric address that says which network it is on, so gateways only need to know how to reach networks, not individual machines. No central authority runs the whole thing; cooperation rests on shared standards published openly as RFCs.`,history:`In 1981 Jon Postel set out the plan to move every ARPANET host to TCP/IP by a single deadline. On 1 January 1983, flag day, the ARPANET's old NCP protocol was switched off, and hosts that had not converted were cut off. In October 1983 the military split its sites off into a separate network, MILNET, joined to the rest by gateways. In 1983 Berkeley's version of Unix shipped with TCP/IP built in, putting it on many university computers. In 1984 the number of internet hosts passed 1,000, and in 1987 it passed 10,000. In 1990 the original ARPANET was shut down.`,facts:[`Internet hosts passed 100,000 in 1989 and 1 million in 1992.`,`Some engineers wore badges reading 'I survived the TCP transition' (1983).`]},"tech:dns":{intro:`The Domain Name System turns names people can remember into the numeric addresses computers use. Instead of one master file listing every computer, a tree of servers shares the job: each organisation answers for its own names, and anyone can ask. It can grow as fast as the network does.`,how:`Names are read right to left, from a top-level domain such as edu or com down to a single computer. A resolver asks a root server who handles the top-level domain, asks that server who handles the next part, and so on until it gets an address, then remembers the answers for a while to save asking again. Every zone has more than one server, so a single failure does not break it.`,history:`In the 1970s every ARPANET computer kept a copy of one file, HOSTS.TXT, maintained at SRI, which grew harder to manage as the network grew. In November 1983 Paul Mockapetris published the design of the Domain Name System in RFC 882 and RFC 883. In 1984 four Berkeley graduate students wrote BIND, a DNS server for Unix. In January 1985 the first top-level domains, such as com, edu, org and gov, came into use, and on 15 March 1985 symbolics.com became the first registered com name. In 1987 RFC 1034 and RFC 1035 replaced the first specification. In 1998 ICANN was created to oversee the system.`,facts:[`In 1995 Network Solutions began charging $100 to register a name for two years.`,`HOSTS.TXT listed every ARPANET computer by name and number (1982).`]},"tech:router":{intro:`A multiprotocol router is a dedicated box that reads the address on each packet and sends it towards the right network. It can join networks that speak different protocols and use different cables, keep them apart where needed, and choose among several routes. Campuses and companies can link their many local networks to each other and to the outside world.`,how:`The router keeps a table of which networks lie beyond each of its connections, learned by swapping messages with neighbouring routers. For every packet it looks up the destination, picks the best outgoing link and forwards it in a fraction of a millisecond. Doing this in software on a fast processor lets one box handle IP, Xerox, Novell and AppleTalk traffic side by side.`,history:`In 1981 Stanford engineers built a 'blue box' to connect the university's separate networks, with hardware by Andy Bechtolsheim and software by William Yeager. In December 1984 Len Bosack and Sandy Lerner, who ran networks in different Stanford departments, founded Cisco Systems. In 1986 Cisco shipped its first commercial router, the AGS. In February 1990 Cisco sold its shares to the public. In 1998 Juniper Networks challenged Cisco with routers that forwarded packets using custom chips. In March 2000, at the peak of the internet boom, Cisco was briefly the most valuable company in the world.`,facts:[`Bosack and Lerner were a married couple who ran rival Stanford networks (1984).`,`Cisco was worth more than $500 billion at its peak in March 2000.`]},"tech:nsfnet":{intro:`The NSFNET is a fast national backbone network paid for by the US National Science Foundation. It links the new supercomputer centres to each other and to regional networks that serve universities across the country. Any research campus that joins a regional network can reach every other one through it.`,how:`The backbone is a set of routers at major sites joined by leased lines. Regional networks connect to it, and campuses connect to the regionals, a three-level design that spreads the cost and the work. Its rules allow only research and education traffic, so companies must build their own networks for business use.`,history:`In 1985 the National Science Foundation announced five supercomputer centres, and in 1986 the NSFNET linked them at 56 kilobits a second. In 1987 the foundation awarded a contract to Merit, IBM and MCI to rebuild the backbone, and in July 1988 it was upgraded to T1 lines at 1.5 megabits a second. In 1991 the backbone moved to 45-megabit lines. In 1992 Congress allowed commercial traffic on networks the foundation funded. On 30 April 1995 the NSFNET backbone was shut down, and commercial providers carried the internet instead.`,facts:[`The first NSFNET backbone ran at 56 kilobits a second (1986).`,`Its 1991 lines were about 800 times faster than its 1986 ones.`]},"tech:bbs":{intro:`A bulletin board system, or BBS, is a home computer with a modem that answers the telephone. Callers dial in one at a time to read and leave messages, play simple games and swap files. Anyone with a spare phone line can run one from a bedroom, and each board becomes a small community.`,how:`The BBS program answers the ring, asks for a name and password, and shows menus as text over the slow modem link. Messages are stored on floppy or hard disks for the next caller to read. Because only one person can use a line at a time, popular boards are often engaged, and long-distance calls cost money, so most callers stick to boards in their own area.`,history:`On 16 February 1978, after a Chicago blizzard kept them indoors, Ward Christensen and Randy Suess put CBBS, the first computer bulletin board, online. In 1977 Christensen had already written XMODEM, a way to send files over modems with error checking, which boards used widely. In 1984 Tom Jennings started FidoNet, which passed messages from board to board by phoning at night when calls were cheap. In 1985 the Whole Earth 'Lectronic Link, the WELL, opened in California. By 1994 the US had about 60,000 boards with some 17 million users.`,facts:[`FidoNet boards swapped mail during a nightly 'mail hour' (1984).`,`About 60,000 American boards were running in 1994.`]},"tech:smartmodem":{intro:`A smart modem has its own little processor, so the computer can control it by sending simple text commands. It dials numbers, answers incoming calls, sets speeds and hangs up when told. Any computer with a serial port and a terminal program can go online without special hardware or a separate telephone.`,how:`Commands start with the letters AT, for attention: ATDT dials a number with tones, ATA answers and ATH hangs up. When the call connects, the modem switches from command mode to passing data. To get back to commands without hanging up, the computer sends three plus signs with a pause either side, which ordinary data rarely does. The modem reports back with words such as CONNECT and NO CARRIER.`,history:`In 1977 Dennis Hayes and Dale Heatherington began building modems for hobby computers in Atlanta, and in 1978 they founded Hayes Microcomputer Products. In 1981 the Hayes Smartmodem went on sale, a 300-bit-a-second modem controlled by AT commands through a standard serial port. In 1982 a 1,200-bit Smartmodem followed. In 1985 Hayes won a patent on the pause around the escape sequence, and rival makers paid licence fees. In 1998 Hayes, undercut by cheaper makers, filed for bankruptcy.`,facts:[`By 1985 Hayes's AT commands were copied by almost every modem maker.`,`A 1,200-bit modem of 1982 sent a page of text in about 15 seconds.`]},"tech:fastmodem":{intro:`Fast modems squeeze many more bits through the same narrow telephone channel by packing several bits into each change of the tone. Clever coding lets them correct errors as they go. Speeds climb from a few hundred bits a second towards the theoretical limit of an ordinary phone line.`,how:`Instead of two pitches, the modem changes both the strength and the timing of its tone thousands of times a second, so each change carries several bits. Trellis coding adds a pattern of redundancy that lets the receiver pick the most likely message even through noise. Both modems test the line when they connect and choose the fastest speed it will bear. Claude Shannon's formula sets the ceiling for a line of given bandwidth and noise.`,history:`In 1948 Claude Shannon proved that every channel has a maximum rate it can carry without errors, set by its bandwidth and noise. In 1982 Gottfried Ungerboeck published trellis-coded modulation, which made higher modem speeds reliable. In 1984 the CCITT standard V.32 set out 9,600 bits a second in both directions over dial-up lines. In 1986 US Robotics sold its own 9,600-bit HST modems to bulletin boards. In 1991 V.32bis reached 14,400 bits a second, and in 1994 V.34 reached 28,800. In 1996 a revised V.34 managed 33,600 bits a second.`,facts:[`A V.34 modem was nearly 100 times faster than a Bell 103 (1994).`,`Shannon's 1948 formula put an ordinary phone line's ceiling at about 35,000 bits a second.`]},"tech:v90":{intro:`A 56k modem gets nearly twice the download speed of the fastest ordinary modems. It works because the internet provider's end of the call is already digital: only the stretch of copper from the exchange to the home is analogue, so most of the noise that limits ordinary modems is avoided on the way down.`,how:`The provider's equipment sends exact digital levels into the telephone network, which the exchange turns into voltages on the home line. The home modem measures those voltages and works out which levels were sent, 8,000 times a second. Uploads travel the hard way, at no more than 33,600 bits a second. Line quality, extra conversions and power limits usually keep speeds below the full 56,000.`,history:`In February 1997 US Robotics launched its x2 modems, and a rival group led by Rockwell and Lucent launched K56flex, which could not talk to x2. In 1997 buyers hesitated while the two camps fought, and internet providers had to pick a side. In February 1998 the ITU agreed a single standard, V.90, which was formally approved in September 1998. In 2000 V.92 sped up uploads and let the modem pause for an incoming phone call. By 2000 most new home PCs came with a 56k modem built in.`,facts:[`US limits on line power held 56k modems to about 53,300 bits a second (1998).`,`At typical speeds a 1 MB file took about three minutes to download (1998).`]},"tech:isp":{intro:`Commercial internet access lets anyone with a computer and a modem rent a connection to the whole internet, paying by the month or the hour. Until now the network has been reached mainly from universities, government laboratories and big companies. An internet service provider answers the customer's call, gives them an address and passes their traffic to the wider net.`,how:`The provider runs banks of modems answering local phone numbers, plus servers for email and news, and buys a link onto a larger network. Customers dial in with their own modems and log in with a user name and password. Providers swap traffic at shared meeting points, so a customer of one can reach a customer of any other.`,history:`In 1987 Rick Adams founded UUNET in Virginia, selling access to Usenet news and email. In 1989 The World in Brookline, Massachusetts, began selling dial-up internet access to the public, and that year MCI Mail and CompuServe connected their email services to the internet. In 1991 commercial providers founded the Commercial Internet Exchange to swap traffic freely among themselves. In September 1993 AOL opened Usenet to its members, and the flood of newcomers became known as the 'Eternal September'. In December 1996 AOL switched to unlimited use for $19.95 a month, and its phone lines jammed for months.`,facts:[`PSINet, founded in 1989, was one of the first commercial internet backbone firms.`,`In 1989 MCI Mail became the first commercial email service linked to the internet.`]},"tech:www":{intro:`The World Wide Web joins documents on computers all over the internet into a single web of pages. Each page has an address, a URL, and can link to any other page anywhere with a click. Pages are written in a simple markup language, HTML, and fetched with an equally simple protocol, HTTP.`,how:`A web server holds pages and sends one whenever a browser asks for its URL. The browser shows the text and turns marked words into links; following one sends a new request, perhaps to a different server on another continent. No central index or permission is needed: anyone can publish a page, and anyone can link to it.`,history:`In March 1989 Tim Berners-Lee at CERN, the European physics laboratory near Geneva, wrote a proposal for an information system to help its scientists share documents. In 1990 he and Robert Cailliau refined the plan, and by December 1990 Berners-Lee had the first web server and a browser-editor running on a NeXT computer. On 6 August 1991 he announced the web publicly on an internet newsgroup. In December 1991 the first web server outside Europe went up at the Stanford Linear Accelerator Center in California. On 30 April 1993 CERN put the web's software into the public domain, free for anyone. In October 1994 Berners-Lee founded the World Wide Web Consortium at MIT.`,facts:[`Berners-Lee's boss wrote 'Vague but exciting' on the 1989 proposal.`,`The first website, at CERN, explained the web itself (1991).`]},"tech:browser":{intro:`A graphical browser shows web pages with pictures and text mixed together, in fonts and layouts, and follows a link with one click of the mouse. It runs on the ordinary personal computers people already own. The web stops being a tool for scientists and becomes something anyone can explore.`,how:`The browser fetches a page by its URL, reads its HTML and lays out the text, then fetches each picture the page refers to and places it in position. Links are underlined and coloured; clicking one fetches a new page, and back and forward buttons retrace the path. It also understands older internet services, so one program reaches almost everything online.`,history:`In January 1993 Marc Andreessen and Eric Bina at the National Center for Supercomputing Applications in Illinois released Mosaic for Unix, and versions for Windows and the Macintosh followed in 1993. In April 1994 Andreessen and Jim Clark founded the company that became Netscape, and in October 1994 it released its Navigator browser. On 9 August 1995 Netscape's shares more than doubled on their first day of trading. In August 1995 Microsoft released Internet Explorer, and the 'browser wars' began. In 1998 Netscape released its browser's source code, which became the Mozilla project.`,facts:[`About 130 websites existed in June 1993, and about 23,500 by June 1995.`,`Netscape's shares were priced at $28 and closed at about $58 on their first day (1995).`]},"tech:ssl":{intro:`Secure connections let a browser and a web server talk privately across the open internet. They agree on a secret key that no eavesdropper can learn, encrypt everything with it, and check a certificate proving the server is who it claims to be. A card number or password can cross the network safely, and a padlock shows the link is secure.`,how:`The server sends a certificate, signed by a trusted authority, containing its public key. The browser checks the signature, uses public-key cryptography to agree a fresh session key with the server, and both switch to fast ordinary encryption for the rest of the conversation. A checksum on every message reveals tampering. Addresses of secure pages start with https.`,history:`In 1976 Whitfield Diffie and Martin Hellman showed how two strangers could agree a secret key in public, and in 1977 Ron Rivest, Adi Shamir and Leonard Adleman published the RSA method. In 1994 Netscape engineers designed the Secure Sockets Layer, and in 1995 SSL 2.0 shipped in the Navigator browser. In August 1995 a French student broke a 40-bit SSL session, the strongest that US export rules allowed abroad, in about eight days using borrowed computers. In 1996 SSL 3.0 fixed serious flaws in version 2. In January 1999 the IETF published it as TLS 1.0. In 2016 the free certificate authority Let's Encrypt came out of its testing phase.`,facts:[`US export rules limited browsers sold abroad to 40-bit keys (1995).`,`By 2020 most pages loaded in Google's Chrome browser used https.`]},"tech:cdn":{intro:`A content delivery network keeps copies of popular web pages, pictures and videos on thousands of servers placed close to users, inside the networks of many internet providers. A visitor is quietly sent to the nearest copy instead of fetching from one faraway server. Pages load faster, and a sudden crowd cannot knock a site over.`,how:`When a browser looks up a site's name, the delivery network's own name servers reply with the address of a nearby, lightly loaded server. That server holds cached copies of the site's files, fetches anything missing from the original just once, and serves it to everyone nearby. Spreading the load over thousands of machines also shields sites from floods of traffic.`,history:`In 1995 Tim Berners-Lee challenged MIT colleagues to fix the web's congestion, and in 1998 mathematician Tom Leighton and his student Danny Lewin founded Akamai to do it. In 1999 Akamai began commercial service, and in March 1999 it helped deliver the trailer for Star Wars: Episode I to millions of downloaders. In October 1999 Akamai's shares soared on their first day of trading. On 11 September 2001 Danny Lewin was killed aboard American Airlines Flight 11. By 2010 Akamai said it carried between 15 and 30 per cent of the world's web traffic.`,facts:[`Akamai means 'clever' or 'cool' in Hawaiian (1998).`,`Akamai's shares rose more than fivefold on their first trading day in 1999.`]},"tech:cablemodem":{intro:`A cable modem sends internet data over the same coaxial cable that brings television into the home. A spare television channel carries data downstream at millions of bits a second, far faster than a telephone modem, and the connection is always on. The catch is that the whole street shares the same channel.`,how:`At the cable company's hub, a termination system sends data to every home on a segment as a digital signal in a 6-megahertz television channel, and each modem picks out the packets meant for it. Upstream, modems take turns in a narrower, lower band, as the hub allows. Because neighbours share the capacity, speeds drop in busy evening hours, and old one-way cable networks must be upgraded to carry signals both ways.`,history:`In 1995 the @Home Network was founded in California to sell internet access over cable, and in 1996 it began service. In March 1997 CableLabs published DOCSIS 1.0, so modems from any maker could work on any cable system. In 1999 @Home merged with the web portal Excite, and in 2001 Excite@Home went bankrupt. In 2001 DOCSIS 2.0 sped up uploads. In 2006 DOCSIS 3.0 let a modem bond several channels together.`,facts:[`DOCSIS 3.0 bonded channels for speeds over 100 megabits a second (2006).`,`The Excite and @Home merger was worth about $6.7 billion (1999).`]},"tech:dsl":{intro:`DSL, digital subscriber line, sends fast data over the ordinary copper telephone wire already running into every home. A voice call uses only the lowest few thousand cycles a second of what the wire can carry; DSL uses the much higher frequencies above them. One line carries a phone call and an always-on internet connection at once.`,how:`Asymmetric DSL splits the high frequencies into hundreds of narrow sub-channels, each carrying as many bits as its noise allows, and gives most of them to downloads, since home users receive far more than they send. A small filter at each phone keeps the two signals apart. Speed falls with distance: homes more than a few kilometres from the exchange get little or nothing.`,history:`In 1988 Joseph Lechleider at Bellcore suggested making the line asymmetric, faster towards the home, to avoid interference between wires in the same cable. In 1993 American telephone companies tested ADSL as a way to deliver video on demand. In 1999 the ITU approved its ADSL standards, including a 'lite' version that needed no engineer to install. In 2000 BT began selling ADSL in Britain. By 2005 DSL was the most common kind of broadband in the world. In 2006 VDSL2 used shorter lines from street cabinets to reach tens of megabits a second.`,facts:[`ADSL used 256 sub-channels, each about 4 kilohertz wide (1999).`,`Early British ADSL ran at 512 kilobits a second (2000).`]},"tech:wifi":{intro:`Wi-Fi is Ethernet without the cable: computers send their network data by radio in a band anyone may use without a licence. A small base station plugged into the wired network lets laptops connect anywhere in a home, office or café. At 11 megabits a second it is as fast as many wired office networks.`,how:`Wi-Fi uses the 2.4-gigahertz band, shared with microwave ovens and cordless phones, and spreads each signal so it survives interference. Because a radio cannot hear its own collisions, each station listens, waits a random time before sending and expects an acknowledgement for every frame. The base station bridges the wireless and wired networks, and encryption is meant to stop neighbours listening in.`,history:`In 1985 the US Federal Communications Commission opened some radio bands for unlicensed spread-spectrum use. In 1991 NCR's WaveLAN, developed in the Netherlands, offered wireless networking for shop tills and offices. In 1997 the IEEE's 802.11 standard, its committee chaired by Vic Hayes, set out wireless networking at 2 megabits a second. In 1999 802.11b raised it to 11, the Wi-Fi Alliance formed to test that equipment worked together, and Apple's iBook offered an AirPort card. In 2001 researchers showed that Wi-Fi's original WEP encryption could be broken. In 2003 802.11g reached 54 megabits a second.`,facts:[`Apple's AirPort card cost $99 and its base station $299 (1999).`,`The name Wi-Fi was invented by a branding firm in 1999.`]},"tech:voip":{intro:`Voice over IP sends telephone conversations as packets over the internet instead of along circuits through the telephone network. The voice is digitised, compressed and sent in small chunks, like any other data. A call to another continent can cost no more than browsing the web, and computers can call each other free.`,how:`Software or a small adaptor samples the voice, compresses it and sends a packet every 20 milliseconds or so. The receiver holds a few packets in a buffer to smooth out uneven delays and fills in for any that are lost. Gateways connect internet calls to ordinary phone numbers. Calls sound poor if the delay passes a few hundred milliseconds.`,history:`In 1995 the first internet phone software let PC users talk over the internet with a microphone and patience. In 2002 Vonage began selling home phone service over broadband in the US. In August 2003 Niklas Zennström and Janus Friis launched Skype, written by programmers in Estonia, which used peer-to-peer technology to connect calls without big central servers. In 2005 eBay bought Skype for about $2.6 billion, and in 2011 Microsoft bought it for $8.5 billion. In May 2025 Microsoft shut Skype down in favour of Teams.`,facts:[`Skype's Estonian programmers had written the Kazaa file-sharing program in 2001.`,`Skype had about 170 million regular users when Microsoft bought it in 2011.`]},"tech:ftth":{intro:`Fibre to the home runs optical fibre all the way into each house instead of stopping at the exchange or a street cabinet. Glass carries light with almost no loss over many kilometres, so homes get hundreds of megabits a second, and much more whenever the boxes at each end are upgraded. The costly part is digging up every street.`,how:`Most systems use a passive optical network: one fibre leaves the exchange and is divided by unpowered glass splitters to serve up to 32 or 64 homes. Every home receives the downstream light, encrypted separately for each, and they take turns sending upstream on another colour. A small box in the house turns the light into Ethernet and phone connections.`,history:`In 2001 Japan's NTT began selling fibre-to-the-home broadband, and in 2008 fibre overtook DSL as Japan's most popular kind of broadband. In 2003 the ITU set the GPON standard for passive optical networks. In 2004 Verizon launched its FiOS fibre internet service in Keller, Texas, and in 2005 it added television. In 2012 Google Fiber offered 1-gigabit service in Kansas City for $70 a month. By 2020 China had hundreds of millions of homes connected by fibre.`,facts:[`A GPON fibre carried 2.5 gigabits a second downstream, shared between homes (2003).`,`Verizon's first FiOS plans started at 5 megabits a second (2004).`]},"tech:laser":{intro:`A laser makes light whose waves march in step, all of one colour and travelling in one direction. The beam stays narrow over long distances and can be focused to a tiny, intense spot. Scientists imagine uses in measurement, communication and medicine, though for now some call it 'a solution looking for a problem'.`,how:`Atoms in a crystal or gas are pumped with energy, from a flash lamp or an electric current, until more are excited than relaxed. A passing photon then triggers excited atoms to release identical photons, which is called stimulated emission, and mirrors at each end bounce the light back and forth to build it up. One mirror lets a little through, and that is the beam.`,history:`In 1917 Albert Einstein predicted stimulated emission of light. In 1954 Charles Townes built the maser, which did the same with microwaves. In 1957 Gordon Gould, a graduate student, coined the word 'laser' in his notebooks, and in 1958 Townes and Arthur Schawlow described how an optical maser might work. On 16 May 1960 Theodore Maiman at Hughes Research Laboratories in California fired the first laser, a rod of synthetic ruby lit by a spiral flash lamp. In December 1960 Ali Javan's team at Bell Labs ran the first continuous gas laser, using helium and neon. In 1964 Townes shared the Nobel Prize in Physics with Nikolay Basov and Aleksandr Prokhorov.`,facts:[`Maiman's ruby rod was only about 2 centimetres long (1960).`,`In 1974 an Ohio supermarket made the first barcode scan with a laser scanner.`]},"tech:laserdiode":{intro:`A laser diode is a laser made from a tiny chip of semiconductor, such as gallium arsenide. A current flowing through it makes light directly, and the light can be switched on and off billions of times a second just by changing the current. It is small, efficient and cheap to make in batches, like a transistor.`,how:`Electrons and holes meet at a junction between two kinds of semiconductor and give up their energy as light. Sandwiching a thin active layer between materials with different properties, a heterostructure, traps both the electric charges and the light in that layer, so far less current is needed. The polished ends of the chip act as mirrors. Heat and wear limit its life.`,history:`In 1962 teams at General Electric, IBM and MIT's Lincoln Laboratory independently made the first semiconductor lasers, but they worked only in short pulses while chilled with liquid nitrogen. In 1963 Herbert Kroemer and Zhores Alferov each proposed heterostructures to solve the problem. In 1970 Alferov's team at the Ioffe Institute in Leningrad, and Izuo Hayashi and Morton Panish at Bell Labs, made laser diodes that ran continuously at room temperature. In 1970 these first continuous laser diodes wore out after a short time. In 1982 compact disc players put a laser diode in millions of homes. In 2000 Alferov and Kroemer shared the Nobel Prize in Physics.`,facts:[`CD players read discs with an infrared laser diode at 780 nanometres (1982).`,`The first laser diodes of 1962 had to be cooled to the temperature of liquid nitrogen.`]},"tech:fibre":{intro:`Low-loss optical fibre is glass so pure that light can travel through kilometres of it and still be bright enough to detect. A hair-thin strand guides light along a core surrounded by glass that bends it back inside. The glass itself was never the problem; the impurities in it were.`,how:`The core has a slightly higher refractive index than the cladding around it, so light striking the boundary at a shallow angle reflects back in. Losses are measured in decibels per kilometre: 20 decibels means one per cent of the light remains after a kilometre. The fibre is made by depositing ultra-pure glass from vapour inside a tube, collapsing it into a rod and drawing the rod into a thread.`,history:`In 1966 Charles Kao and George Hockham at Standard Telecommunication Laboratories in Harlow, England, argued that glass fibre could carry communications if its losses fell below 20 decibels a kilometre. In 1970 Robert Maurer, Donald Keck and Peter Schultz at Corning Glass Works in New York state made a fibre that beat that target. In 1972 Corning reached about 4 decibels a kilometre. In 1979 researchers at Japan's NTT reported losses of only about 0.2 decibels a kilometre at longer infrared wavelengths. In 2009 Kao shared the Nobel Prize in Physics.`,facts:[`Corning's 1970 fibre lost about 17 decibels a kilometre.`,`At 0.2 decibels a kilometre, half the light survives 15 kilometres of glass (1979).`]},"tech:fibretrunk":{intro:`Fibre-optic trunks carry telephone calls between exchanges as pulses of light in hair-thin glass. A single fibre carries hundreds of calls, a cable holds many fibres, and light is immune to electrical interference. Fibre is thinner and lighter than copper, so it fits into the crowded ducts under city streets.`,how:`A laser or light-emitting diode flashes the digital signal into the fibre, and a photodetector at the far end turns it back into electrical pulses. Repeaters along the route detect, clean up and resend the signal, but they can sit kilometres apart, much farther than copper allows. Joining fibres needs precise alignment, so installation crews need new skills and tools.`,history:`On 22 April 1977 General Telephone and Electronics carried the first live telephone traffic over fibre in Long Beach, California, at 6 megabits a second. In May 1977 the Bell System opened a fibre link under downtown Chicago carrying voice, data and video. In 1977 Britain's Post Office also began carrying live calls over optical fibre. In 1983 AT&T began building a fibre route along the Northeast Corridor from Boston to Washington. In 1986 Sprint advertised that its all-fibre network was so quiet you could hear a pin drop.`,facts:[`The 1977 Chicago fibre link ran at 45 megabits a second.`,`The Chicago link of 1977 was about 2.4 kilometres long.`]},"tech:subfibre":{intro:`Undersea fibre lays glass fibres across the ocean floor, with electronic regenerators every few dozen kilometres to rebuild the fading light pulses. One cable carries far more calls than any copper cable before it, without the half-second echo of a satellite link. The cost of a call across an ocean begins to fall.`,how:`Pairs of fibres sit at the centre of a cable armoured against anchors and trawlers. Regenerators sealed in pressure-proof cases turn the light back into electricity, clean up the pulses and resend them with a fresh laser, powered by a high-voltage current fed from the shore. Each one must work for 25 years on the sea bed.`,history:`On 14 December 1988 TAT-8 entered service between the US, Britain and France, the first transatlantic fibre cable, built by AT&T, British Telecom and France Télécom. In 1988 TAT-8 could carry about 40,000 telephone circuits at once. In 1989 TPC-3, the first fibre cable across the Pacific, linked Japan with Hawaii. By 1990 TAT-8 was almost full, much sooner than its owners had planned, and new cables were ordered. In 2002 TAT-8 was retired.`,facts:[`TAT-8 cost about $335 million (1988).`,`TAT-8 sent its light pulses at a wavelength of 1.3 micrometres (1988).`]},"tech:edfa":{intro:`An optical amplifier strengthens light directly as it travels down a fibre, with no need to turn it into electricity and back. A few metres of fibre laced with the rare element erbium, energised by a small pump laser, boosts every signal passing through, whatever its speed or colour. Long cables no longer need electronics matched to their exact data rate.`,how:`The pump laser lifts erbium atoms into an excited state; passing signal photons in the 1.55-micrometre band stimulate them to release identical photons, adding strength to the signal. Because the amplifier never reads the data, the same amplifiers keep working when the equipment at the ends is upgraded to faster rates or more colours. Noise builds up slowly along the chain, so very long links need careful design.`,history:`In 1986 and 1987 David Payne's group at the University of Southampton and Emmanuel Desurvire's team at Bell Labs showed working erbium-doped fibre amplifiers. In 1996 TAT-12 and TAT-13 became the first transatlantic cables to use optical amplifiers instead of electronic regenerators, each fibre pair carrying 5 gigabits a second. In 1997 the FLAG cable, built with optical amplifiers, opened between Britain and Japan. By 2000 amplified links carried dozens of colours on each fibre.`,facts:[`Erbium amplifiers work around 1.55 micrometres, where glass fibre loses least light (1987).`,`The FLAG cable of 1997 stretched about 28,000 kilometres from Britain to Japan.`]},"tech:dwdm":{intro:`Dense wavelength-division multiplexing sends many beams of slightly different colours down one fibre at once, each carrying its own stream of data. The colours do not interfere, so one fibre does the work of dozens, and one optical amplifier boosts them all together. Capacity can be added by lighting new colours instead of laying new cable.`,how:`Lasers tuned to precise wavelengths, spaced less than a nanometre apart, each carry one channel. A multiplexer of filters or gratings combines them into the fibre, and at the far end a demultiplexer separates them onto their own detectors. Because erbium amplifiers boost a whole band at once, a long route needs one amplifier per span, not one per colour. The lasers are held at steady temperatures so their colours do not drift.`,history:`In 1992 Ciena was founded in Maryland to build wavelength-division systems. In June 1996 Ciena shipped its first DWDM system to Sprint, sending 16 colours of 2.5 gigabits a second each down a single fibre. In February 1997 Ciena's shares began trading in one of the biggest stock market debuts of its time. In 2001 laboratory demonstrations sent more than 10 terabits a second down one fibre. In 2001 and 2002 the telecom bubble burst, leaving vast numbers of unused fibres in the ground.`,facts:[`Ciena's first system carried 40 gigabits a second on one fibre (1996).`,`The ITU's standard grid of 2002 spaced channels 100 gigahertz apart.`]},"tech:coherent":{intro:`Coherent optics reads the phase of the light, the exact timing of its waves, as well as its brightness, in two polarisations at once. Each pulse then carries several bits, and powerful signal-processing chips undo the smearing a fibre adds over long distances. One colour of light carries 100 gigabits a second.`,how:`At the receiver the incoming light is mixed with light from a local laser, so detectors can measure both phase and strength. Very fast converters turn this into numbers, and a digital signal processor calculates and reverses the distortions caused by the fibre, replacing reels of special compensating fibre. A format such as DP-QPSK sends four bits in every symbol, two in each polarisation.`,history:`In the 1980s laboratories studied coherent fibre receivers, but erbium amplifiers made simpler systems good enough and the work stalled. In 2008 Nortel shipped coherent equipment at 40 gigabits a second, made practical by fast digital chips. In December 2009 Verizon put a coherent 100-gigabit channel into service between Paris and Frankfurt. In 2010 Ciena completed its purchase of Nortel's optical business. From 2010 network operators around the world installed coherent 100G on their long-distance routes.`,facts:[`DP-QPSK carries four bits in each symbol (2010).`,`Ciena paid about $769 million for Nortel's optical business (2010).`]},"tech:cellular":{intro:`Cellular phones divide a city into small areas, cells, each served by its own low-power tower. The same radio channels can be reused a few cells away without interfering, so thousands of people can talk at once on a handful of frequencies. When a car crosses into a new cell, the call is handed over without a break.`,how:`Each cell's base station uses a set of channels different from its neighbours'. A central switching centre tracks which cell each phone is in by measuring signal strength, and moves the call to a new channel and tower as the phone travels. Smaller cells mean more reuse and more capacity, at the price of more towers. Calls are analogue radio, so anyone with a scanner can listen in.`,history:`In December 1947 Bell Labs engineers Douglas Ring and Rae Young described the cellular idea in an internal memo. On 3 April 1973 Motorola's Martin Cooper made a public call on a handheld prototype in New York. In December 1979 NTT opened the first commercial cellular network in Tokyo. In 1981 the Nordic NMT system opened, working across Sweden, Norway, Denmark and Finland. On 13 October 1983 Ameritech launched the first US cellular service, AMPS, in Chicago. In 1984 Motorola's DynaTAC 8000X handheld phone sold for $3,995.`,facts:[`The DynaTAC weighed about 0.8 kilograms and gave 30 minutes of talk (1984).`,`Cooper's 1973 call went to a rival engineer at Bell Labs.`]},"tech:gsm":{intro:`GSM is a single digital mobile phone standard shared by a whole continent. Calls are turned into compressed digital data and encrypted, so scanners cannot eavesdrop, and each subscriber's identity sits on a removable SIM card. A phone bought in one country works in others, through agreements between networks.`,how:`Each radio channel is divided into eight time slots, so one frequency carries eight calls in turn. A speech coder squeezes each voice into about 13 kilobits a second. The SIM holds the subscriber's secret key, which the network checks on every connection. Roaming agreements let visiting phones register on foreign networks, and the bills follow them home.`,history:`In 1982 the European conference of postal and telecoms administrations set up a working group, the Groupe Spécial Mobile, to design a common digital system. In 1987 network operators from 13 countries signed an agreement to build it. On 1 July 1991 Finland's prime minister Harri Holkeri made the first GSM call, on the Radiolinja network. In 1992 the first international roaming agreement was signed, between Telecom Finland and Britain's Vodafone. By 1995 GSM had about 10 million users, and in 2004 it passed 1 billion.`,facts:[`The first GSM networks used the 900-megahertz band (1991).`,`GSM passed 1 billion users in 2004, more than any other phone system.`]},"tech:sms":{intro:`Text messages are short notes typed on a mobile phone and delivered to another phone within seconds. They travel in the spare room of the signalling channel the network already uses to set up calls, so they cost the operator almost nothing to carry. Each message holds up to 160 characters, about the length of a postcard.`,how:`The phone sends the message to a message centre in the network, which stores it and forwards it as soon as the recipient's phone can be reached, even if it was switched off when the message was sent. Because messages ride the control channel, they can arrive during a call. Typing uses the number keys, pressing each one several times to pick a letter.`,history:`In 1985 Friedhelm Hillebrand, a German engineer, typed out sample sentences and decided that 160 characters were enough for most messages. On 3 December 1992 Neil Papworth sent 'Merry Christmas' from a computer to the phone of Vodafone director Richard Jarvis in Britain. In 1993 the first phone-to-phone text messages were sent in Finland. In 1999 British networks let customers text people on rival networks, and texting soared. In 2010 the world sent about 6.1 trillion text messages.`,facts:[`Papworth sent the 1992 message from a computer, because phones could not yet type them.`,`The 6.1 trillion texts of 2010 averaged nearly 200,000 every second.`]},"tech:cdma":{intro:`CDMA, code-division multiple access, lets every phone in an area talk at once on the same wide band of frequencies. Each call is mixed with its own long code, and the receiver uses that code to pick one conversation out of the hiss of all the others, like hearing one language at a noisy party. It squeezes more calls into the same spectrum.`,how:`Each bit of speech is multiplied by a fast code sequence that spreads it across about 1.25 megahertz. The receiver multiplies by the same code, which gathers the wanted signal back while everyone else's stays spread out as faint noise. Phones must constantly adjust their power so near ones do not drown out far ones, and a phone can talk to two towers at once while being handed over.`,history:`In 1942 actress Hedy Lamarr and composer George Antheil patented frequency hopping, an early spread-spectrum idea for guiding torpedoes by radio. In 1985 Irwin Jacobs and Andrew Viterbi founded Qualcomm in San Diego. In 1989 Qualcomm demonstrated CDMA for mobile phones, while many engineers doubted it could work. In 1993 the US adopted CDMA as the IS-95 standard. In 1995 Hutchison launched the first commercial CDMA network in Hong Kong, and in 1996 South Korea adopted CDMA nationwide. In 1999 Qualcomm's shares rose more than twentyfold, carried by royalties from its CDMA patents.`,facts:[`Lamarr and Antheil's 1942 design hopped between 88 frequencies, like piano keys.`,`Qualcomm's shares rose about 2,600 per cent during 1999.`]},"tech:mobiledata":{intro:`Mobile internet brings email, news, weather and simple web pages to a phone's small screen. Data travels in packets over the phone network, so the phone can stay connected all day and pay only for what it sends and receives. Operators sell ringtones, pictures and games through their own menus.`,how:`Packet radio shares the network's time slots among many users, sending data only when there is something to send, instead of holding a circuit open. Pages are written in simplified formats suited to tiny screens and slow links of tens of kilobits a second. The operator controls the portal, takes a share of each sale and adds it to the phone bill.`,history:`In 1997 Nokia, Ericsson, Motorola and Unwired Planet began work on WAP, a way to show simplified web pages on phones. On 22 February 1999 NTT DoCoMo launched i-mode in Japan, with email and services billed through the phone account. In 1999 the first WAP phones, such as the Nokia 7110, went on sale in Europe, but users found them slow and costly. In 2000 GPRS networks opened in Europe, sending packets over GSM. By the end of 2000 i-mode had more than 10 million subscribers in Japan.`,facts:[`DoCoMo kept about 9 per cent of what i-mode content providers charged (1999).`,`Early GPRS ran at a few tens of kilobits a second (2000).`]},"tech:umts":{intro:`3G phones use wideband CDMA radio to give each user hundreds of kilobits a second: enough for real web pages, maps, music downloads and video calls on a phone. The networks are designed for data as well as voice from the start. Governments sell new radio bands for it, and phone companies pay enormous sums to get them.`,how:`Each channel is 5 megahertz wide, four times a CDMA voice channel, and users share it by code. The network adjusts each user's power and data rate many times a second, giving more speed to phones with good signals. 3G phones fall back to GSM wherever there is no 3G coverage.`,history:`In December 1998 standards bodies formed the 3GPP partnership to write a common 3G standard based on wideband CDMA. In April 2000 Britain's auction of 3G licences raised about £22.5 billion, and in August 2000 Germany's raised about €50 billion. On 1 October 2001 NTT DoCoMo launched FOMA in Tokyo, the first commercial wideband CDMA service. In March 2003 Hutchison's '3' launched in Britain and Italy. In 2006 HSDPA upgrades raised download speeds to several megabits a second. In July 2008 Apple's iPhone 3G sold a million in its first weekend.`,facts:[`Britain's 3G auction of 2000 raised nearly £400 for every person in the country.`,`FOMA launched in 2001 with phones that could make video calls.`]},"tech:lte":{intro:`4G LTE is a mobile network built entirely on internet technology: voice, video and data all travel as IP packets. It uses OFDM, the many-narrow-carriers trick of Wi-Fi and DSL, which copes well with radio echoes off buildings. Phones get tens of megabits a second with short delays, enough for video and live maps.`,how:`The radio channel, up to 20 megahertz wide, is split into hundreds of narrow sub-carriers, and the base station hands out blocks of them every millisecond, choosing the best ones for each user. Several antennas at each end send separate streams at once, a technique called MIMO. The core network is a simplified, all-packet design, and voice calls become a data service.`,history:`In December 2008 the 3GPP completed Release 8, the first LTE standard. On 14 December 2009 TeliaSonera opened the first commercial LTE networks in Stockholm and Oslo. In December 2010 Verizon launched LTE in 38 US cities. In August 2012 South Korean operators began carrying voice calls over LTE, and in September 2012 Apple's iPhone 5 added LTE. In April 2019 South Korea launched the first 5G phone networks.`,facts:[`TeliaSonera's first LTE customers in 2009 used laptop modems, because no phones were ready.`,`LTE schedules its radio in slices of 1 millisecond (2009).`]},"tech:orbitlaunch":{intro:`Orbital launch lifts a satellite above the air and sends it sideways at about 8 kilometres a second, so fast that as it falls, the Earth's surface curves away beneath it. It keeps falling around the planet instead of onto it. Rockets built to carry nuclear warheads can do the job.`,how:`A multi-stage rocket burns most of its fuel lifting itself through the thick lower air, then drops empty stages to save weight as it tips over to fly sideways. Each stage adds speed until the payload reaches orbital velocity. Only a few per cent of the launch weight reaches orbit, and rockets often explode, so each kilogram in space costs a fortune.`,history:`In 1903 Konstantin Tsiolkovsky worked out the rocket equation, showing how much fuel a rocket needs to reach a given speed. On 4 October 1957 the Soviet Union launched Sputnik 1 on an R-7 missile designed by Sergei Korolev, and its radio beeps were heard around the world. In November 1957 Sputnik 2 carried the dog Laika. On 6 December 1957 the first American attempt, Vanguard TV3, exploded on the launch pad. On 31 January 1958 Explorer 1 reached orbit and discovered the Van Allen radiation belts. On 12 April 1961 Yuri Gagarin became the first person in orbit.`,facts:[`Sputnik 1 weighed about 84 kilograms and circled the Earth every 96 minutes (1957).`,`Sputnik 1 beeped on 20 and 40 megahertz for about three weeks (1957).`]},"tech:activesat":{intro:`A relay satellite is a radio station in space powered by sunlight. It receives signals from one ground station, amplifies them and sends them down to another far across the ocean. From a low orbit it is in view of both stations for only minutes at a time, so ground antennas must track it across the sky.`,how:`Solar cells charge batteries that run a receiver, an amplifier and a transmitter, working on different frequencies so the outgoing signal does not swamp the incoming one. Huge steerable antennas on the ground follow the satellite as it passes. While it is in view of both sides, a television channel or hundreds of telephone calls can cross an ocean.`,history:`In December 1958 the US satellite SCORE broadcast a recorded Christmas message from President Eisenhower. In August 1960 NASA's Echo 1, a giant metal-coated balloon, bounced signals passively between stations. In October 1960 Courier 1B recorded messages and replayed them as it passed overhead. On 10 July 1962 NASA launched Telstar 1, built by AT&T, and on 23 July 1962 it carried the first public live television across the Atlantic. In 1962 the Tornados' instrumental Telstar topped the charts in Britain and America. In February 1963 Telstar 1 failed, its electronics damaged by radiation from a high-altitude nuclear test.`,facts:[`Telstar 1 weighed about 77 kilograms and carried about 3,600 solar cells (1962).`,`AT&T paid NASA about $3 million for each Telstar launch (1962).`]},"tech:geosat":{intro:`A geostationary satellite circles the Earth once a day, 35,786 kilometres above the equator, so it seems to hang still over one spot. Dishes on the ground can point at it permanently instead of tracking it across the sky. Three such satellites, spaced around the equator, can link almost the whole inhabited world.`,how:`At that height an orbit takes exactly as long as the Earth takes to spin, so the satellite keeps pace with the ground below, and it can see nearly half the planet. Small thrusters keep it on station against the pull of the Moon and Sun. The distance means each signal takes about a quarter of a second to go up and come down, which makes conversations awkward.`,history:`In October 1945 Arthur C Clarke described three relay stations in geostationary orbit in the magazine Wireless World. In July 1963 Hughes Aircraft's Syncom 2, designed by Harold Rosen's team, reached a 24-hour orbit. In August 1964 Syncom 3 became the first truly geostationary satellite, and in October 1964 it relayed the Tokyo Olympics to the US. On 6 April 1965 Intelsat launched Early Bird, the first commercial communications satellite, linking Europe and North America. In July 1969 Intelsat satellites over three oceans carried the Apollo 11 Moon landing to viewers around the world.`,facts:[`Early Bird carried 240 telephone circuits or one television channel (1965).`,`Early Bird was designed to last 18 months but worked for more than three years (1965).`]},"tech:satphone":{intro:`A satellite phone constellation puts dozens of satellites in low orbit so a handheld phone can reach one from anywhere on Earth: an ocean, a desert or a mountain top. Calls pass from satellite to satellite across space and come down at a gateway to the ordinary phone network. No towers are needed on the ground.`,how:`Low orbits, under 800 kilometres up, keep the distance short enough for a pocket phone's small antenna and battery. Each satellite races across the sky in minutes, so calls are handed from one to the next, and links between satellites pass calls along. Building, launching and replacing so many spacecraft costs billions, so handsets and calls are expensive, and phones need a clear view of the sky.`,history:`In 1987 Motorola engineers began planning Iridium, named after the element because the first design called for 77 satellites. In 1997 and 1998 Motorola launched the satellites on American, Russian and Chinese rockets. On 1 November 1998 Iridium began service with 66 working satellites. In August 1999, with far too few customers, Iridium filed for bankruptcy. In 2000 investors bought the network for about $25 million, a tiny fraction of its cost, and the US military became a key customer. Between 2017 and 2019 Iridium replaced the whole constellation with new satellites.`,facts:[`Iridium handsets cost about $3,000, and calls several dollars a minute (1998).`,`Iridium's satellites orbit about 780 kilometres up (1998).`]},"tech:computerroom":{intro:`The computer room is a space built around a machine. Valve computers weigh many tonnes, draw as much power as a street of houses and pour out heat, so they need strong floors, heavy power cables and big air conditioners. The room is kept clean, cool and locked, and run by specialist staff around the clock.`,how:`Each cabinet holds thousands of vacuum tubes that glow like light bulbs, so most of the electricity turns into heat that must be blown away by chilled air. Tubes burn out often, so engineers keep spares and run tests every day. Mercury memories, magnetic drums and tape drives need a steady temperature and little dust, and large rooms have their own transformers.`,history:`In 1946 ENIAC filled a room of about 170 square metres at the University of Pennsylvania and drew about 150 kilowatts. In 1951 the first UNIVAC I was accepted by the US Census Bureau, and in 1952 another UNIVAC predicted Eisenhower's election victory on CBS television. In 1953 IBM began renting out its 701 computer, so customers paid by the month instead of buying. In 1958 each SAGE air-defence computer filled a floor of a concrete blockhouse and needed up to 3 megawatts of power.`,facts:[`UNIVAC I used about 5,000 vacuum tubes and weighed about 13 tonnes (1951).`,`ENIAC had 17,468 vacuum tubes (1946).`]},"tech:raisedfloor":{intro:`A raised floor lifts the computer room's floor on metal legs, leaving a space underneath for cables and cold air. Tiles lift out wherever access is needed, and perforated tiles in front of the machines let cool air rise into them. Air conditioners designed for computers hold the room at a steady temperature and humidity day and night.`,how:`Computer-room air conditioners blow chilled air into the space under the floor, which acts as a pressurised duct. The air rises through vented tiles into the machines and returns, warm, to the units. Precision units control humidity too, since dry air causes static shocks and damp air condensation. Cables can be rearranged without tearing up walls or ceilings, and water pipes for cooled machines run underneath as well.`,history:`In 1964 IBM's System/360 mainframes were installed in rooms on raised floors, with thick cables running beneath. In 1965 Ralph Liebert founded the Liebert Corporation in Columbus, Ohio, and built one of the first air conditioners made for computer rooms. In 1976 the Cray-1 supercomputer was cooled by Freon refrigerant piped through its frame. In 1985 IBM's 3090 mainframes carried away their heat with chilled water. In 2004 the engineering society ASHRAE published its first thermal guidelines for data centres.`,facts:[`The Cray-1 doubled as a bench, with padded seats around its base (1976).`,`Raised floors in mainframe rooms were often 30 to 60 centimetres deep (1965).`]},"tech:timeshare":{intro:`Commercial timesharing rents slices of a big computer to many customers at once. Each one dials in from a terminal, such as a Teletype, and works as if the machine were theirs alone, paying for hours of connection and seconds of processor time. Small firms, schools and scientists can use computers they could never afford to buy.`,how:`The operating system switches between users many times a second, giving each a short slice of processor time and keeping their programs and files apart. Most of the time people are typing or thinking, so one machine can serve dozens. Customers connect through modems over phone lines, and the service bills for connect time, processing and storage.`,history:`In 1959 John McCarthy at MIT and Christopher Strachey in Britain independently proposed time-sharing. In 1961 Fernando Corbató's team at MIT demonstrated CTSS, the Compatible Time-Sharing System. In May 1964 John Kemeny and Thomas Kurtz at Dartmouth College launched their timesharing system together with the BASIC language. In 1965 General Electric began selling timesharing based on the Dartmouth system, and in 1966 Tymshare began its service in California. In 1968 the mothers' club at Lakeside School in Seattle bought time on a GE service, and pupils including Bill Gates learned to program on it. In 1969 CompuServe began as a timesharing business in Ohio.`,facts:[`The first BASIC programs ran at Dartmouth at 4 am on 1 May 1964.`,`A Teletype ASR-33 terminal printed about 10 characters a second (1963).`]},"tech:hotaisle":{intro:`Hot and cold aisles arrange the racks in a computer room so that all their fronts face one aisle and all their backs face the next. Servers draw cool air in at the front and blow hot air out at the back, so cold aisles stay cold and hot aisles carry the heat away. Cooling works far better when the two never mix.`,how:`Perforated floor tiles go only in the cold aisles, where cool air rises in front of the machines. Hot exhaust gathers in the hot aisles and returns to the air conditioners. Blanking panels fill empty slots in the racks so hot air cannot leak back through them. The layout costs nothing but planning, yet it lets the same air conditioners cool far more equipment.`,history:`Robert Sullivan, working at IBM in 1992, is usually credited with proposing the hot-aisle and cold-aisle layout. In the 1990s web hosting centres filled with racks of servers, and the layout spread as heat loads rose. In 2004 ASHRAE's thermal guidelines recommended it. In 2007 The Green Grid introduced power usage effectiveness, PUE, comparing a data centre's total power with what its computers use. By 2010 many operators enclosed their aisles with doors and roofs to stop hot and cold air mixing at all.`,facts:[`ASHRAE's 2004 guidelines recommended server inlet air of 20 to 25 °C.`,`A PUE of 2.0 means half a data centre's power goes on things other than computing (2007).`]},"tech:colocation":{intro:`A colocation centre is a building full of cages and racks, with secure power, cooling and connections to many networks. Customers bring their own servers and rent the space, or rent servers and hosting from the operator. Because many networks meet in one building, traffic can pass between them cheaply.`,how:`The building has diesel generators and battery systems to ride through power cuts, spare cooling, guards and fire suppression. Network providers run fibre into a shared meet-me room, where customers can connect directly to any of them. Prices are charged by rack, by power used and by cross-connect. Web hosting firms add servers, software and support for customers who want no hardware of their own.`,history:`In 1992 MAE-East opened near Washington, one of the first places where internet networks swapped traffic. In 1994 Exodus Communications was founded in California, and in 1998 it sold shares to the public. In 1998 Jay Adelson and Al Avery founded Equinix to build neutral exchange buildings, and in 1999 it opened its first in Ashburn, Virginia. In 2000 Exodus was valued at about $32 billion at the peak of the dot-com boom. In September 2001 Exodus filed for bankruptcy. By 2020 Equinix ran more than 200 data centres around the world.`,facts:[`MAE-East began in a parking garage in northern Virginia (1992).`,`Equinix's name blends equality, neutrality and internet exchange (1998).`]},"tech:commodity":{intro:`Commodity clusters replace a few expensive, ultra-reliable computers with thousands of cheap PCs bought off the shelf. Some of them fail every day, so the software is written to expect it: work and data are spread across many machines and quietly moved when one dies. For the same money, the cluster has far more power.`,how:`Each machine runs an ordinary operating system on cheap processors and disks, often without a case. Software splits each job into many small tasks, sends them to different machines, keeps several copies of every file and reruns anything that fails. Racks are packed tightly, so power and cooling, not the purchase price, become the biggest costs.`,history:`In 1994 Thomas Sterling and Donald Becker at NASA built Beowulf, a supercomputer made of 16 ordinary PCs running Linux. In 1996 Inktomi's search engine ran on clusters of workstations, building on research at Berkeley. In 1999 Google built racks of bare circuit boards mounted on cork boards to save money. In 2003 Google's engineers described clusters of more than 15,000 PCs serving search, and that year published the design of the Google File System. In 2004 Google described MapReduce, and in 2006 the open-source Hadoop copied its ideas.`,facts:[`Beowulf's first cluster of 1994 used 16 Intel 486 processors.`,`A single Google search in 2003 read hundreds of megabytes of data.`]},"tech:servervirt":{intro:`Server virtualisation lets one physical server pretend to be many separate computers. Each virtual machine runs its own operating system and programs as if it had the hardware to itself. Servers that each sat idle most of the day can be combined onto one, saving power, space and money.`,how:`A thin layer of software, the hypervisor, runs directly on the hardware and gives each virtual machine a share of the processor, memory, disks and network. It catches the instructions that touch real hardware and handles them safely. A virtual machine is just files and memory, so it can be copied, backed up or restarted on another server.`,history:`In 1967 IBM's CP-40 research system ran several virtual copies of a mainframe at once, and in 1972 IBM released VM/370 for its System/370 machines. In 1974 Gerald Popek and Robert Goldberg set out the rules a processor must follow to be virtualised efficiently, rules that the PC's x86 chips broke. In 1998 Mendel Rosenblum and colleagues founded VMware, which got round the problem with clever software, and in 2001 it released ESX Server. In 2003 Cambridge researchers released the Xen hypervisor, and in December 2003 EMC agreed to buy VMware. In 2005 and 2006 Intel and AMD added virtualisation support to their processors. In 2006 Amazon's EC2 cloud began renting Xen virtual machines by the hour.`,facts:[`EMC paid about $625 million for VMware (2003).`,`In 2023 Broadcom bought VMware for about $69 billion.`]},"tech:wsc":{intro:`A warehouse-scale computer treats a whole data centre, its buildings, power, cooling, network and tens of thousands of servers, as one machine designed as a single system. Software spreads every service across the fleet, and a small team runs it all. Every efficiency gain is multiplied by the size of the building.`,how:`Designers aim at the total cost of ownership: cheap custom servers, simple power paths with a battery beside each server instead of a big central backup, efficient cooling, and a network that lets any server talk to any other. Cluster software schedules jobs, keeps data in several copies and works around failed machines automatically. Efficiency is tracked as PUE, total power divided by the power that reaches the computers.`,history:`In 2006 Google opened a large data centre at The Dalles, Oregon, beside the Columbia River and its cheap hydroelectric power. In 2007 Microsoft opened a large data centre at Quincy, Washington. In October 2008 Google began publishing its data centres' efficiency, reporting an average PUE of about 1.21. In April 2009 Google showed its custom servers, each with its own battery, and a data centre built from shipping containers. In 2009 Luiz André Barroso and Urs Hölzle published 'The Datacenter as a Computer', the book that named warehouse-scale computers.`,facts:[`A PUE of 1.21 meant about 17 per cent of the power went on overheads (2008).`,`Google's 2009 servers carried a 12-volt battery on each board.`]},"tech:freecooling":{intro:`Free air cooling skips the power-hungry refrigeration plant for most of the year. Data centres in cool climates blow filtered outside air straight through the server halls, or cool it further by evaporating a little water. Servers turn out to be happy at warmer temperatures than anyone had assumed, so chillers are needed only on the hottest days, or not at all.`,how:`Fans draw outside air through filters, mix in warm exhaust air in winter to avoid over-chilling, and push it through the cold aisles. On warm days, water sprayed into the airstream evaporates and cools it. Without compressors, cooling uses a fraction of the energy, and a building's PUE can fall close to 1.1. Sites are chosen for cool, dry climates and clean air.`,history:`In 2008 ASHRAE widened its recommended range for server inlet air to 18 to 27 °C. In 2009 Google opened a data centre at Saint-Ghislain in Belgium with no chillers, cooled by evaporating water drawn from a nearby canal. In 2011 Google converted a former paper mill at Hamina in Finland, cooling it with seawater from the Gulf of Finland. In 2011 Facebook's centre at Prineville, Oregon, was cooled by outside air and evaporation. In 2013 Facebook opened a data centre at Luleå in northern Sweden, near the Arctic Circle.`,facts:[`ASHRAE's 2011 guidelines let some classes of server take in air at up to 45 °C.`,`On rare days too hot for free cooling, Google shifted Saint-Ghislain's work elsewhere (2009).`]},"tech:opencompute":{intro:`Open Compute strips servers, racks and power supplies of everything a big data centre does not need, and publishes the designs for anyone to use and improve. No paint, no logos, no extra parts: just boards that are easy to cool and quick to repair. Big buyers can then have them built cheaply by many manufacturers.`,how:`Designs are released openly, like open-source software, and members contribute improvements. Servers are taller than usual so they can use bigger, slower fans, and power comes from shelves of supplies feeding the whole rack, with fewer conversions on the way. Parts can be swapped without tools from the cold aisle, and factories build to the published specifications.`,history:`In April 2011 Facebook opened its data centre in Prineville, Oregon, and launched the Open Compute Project, publishing its server and building designs. In 2011 the project became a foundation, with members such as Intel and Rackspace. In 2014 Microsoft joined and contributed its own server designs. In 2016 Google joined and offered a 48-volt rack design. By 2020 the project's designs covered network switches, storage and whole racks.`,facts:[`Facebook said Prineville cost 24 per cent less to build and was 38 per cent more efficient (2011).`,`Prineville's PUE was reported at about 1.07 in 2011.`]}};export{e as PEDIA_GAMESNET};